x.update(title='The mass-formula lower bound for rank-32 even unimodular lattices',statement=r'''Let \(\Omega_{32}\) be the integral isometry classes of positive-definite even unimodular lattices of rank 32. The classical mass \(m_{32}=\sum_{L\in\Omega_{32}}|\operatorname{Aut}L|^{-1}\) satisfies \(|\Omega_{32}|\ge\lceil2m_{32}\rceil=80,618,466\), since every such lattice has the two automorphisms \(\pm1\). This is a lower bound, not an exact count.''',reconstruction=r'''Positive definiteness, evenness, integral isometry, and the lower-bound interpretation are restored from the mass formula. The notebook gives only the rounded number and determinant-one condition, so the identification is strong but not textually complete.''',**{'class':'E'},fate=r'''The number is the classical Minkowski--Siegel mass bound. An exact retrospective check is \citet[\S6, comparison following Corollary 17]{king2003mass}, which prints 80,618,466 and obtains the stronger lower bound 1,162,109,024. Its preprint first appeared in 2000 and the paper in 2003; that later improvement is not attributed to the 1972 speaker.''',present_status=r'''As of November 2026: the recovered lower bound is established and has long been surpassed. No assertion of a complete modern enumeration is made.''',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',publication_match='partial',verification='read the result',confidence='medium',refs=['king2003mass'])
