talk(247,'1972-02-21','D. A. Kazhdan','P000400-P000403','Elementary relations and Laurent polynomial K-theory',r'''Kazhdan reviews low algebraic \(K\)-groups and Steinberg relations, then poses the universal-relation question answered in Vaserstein's later March talks. Most of the lecture constructs the Laurent polynomial boundary and its splitting: first through projective modules and polynomial cokernels, then through truncation of two-sided matrices. The notebook itself repairs the matrix class to finite bandwidth. The date and heading occur at P000400; Gabrielov begins below the divider on P000403.''')
item(247,'P000400','definition','Projective K0 and elementary-matrix K1',r'''The opening formulas are \(K_0(A)\) as the Grothendieck group of finitely generated projectives and \(K_1(A)=GL(\infty,A)/E(A)\). A triangular sketch is followed by the explicit statement that \(E(A)\) is generated by all elementary matrices. A Helmholtz colour-subtraction analogy illustrates group completion.''',r'''For a unital associative ring \(A\), \(K_0(A)\) is the group completion of isomorphism classes of finitely generated projective right modules under direct sum. Set \(GL(A)=\varinjlim GL_n(A)\) under \(g\mapsto\operatorname{diag}(g,1)\), and let \(E(A)\) be generated by \(1+ae_{ij}\), \(i\ne j\). Then \(E(A)=[GL(A),GL(A)]\), so \(K_1(A)\) is the abelianization. A single upper triangular subgroup is not \(E(A)\).''',r'''The later generator sentence governs the earlier schematic drawing. The colour analogy is treated as pedagogy, not as a claim of a mathematical identification with physical colour spaces.''','E',r'''The exact stable definitions and commutator result occur in \citet[\S12]{bass1964stable} and \citet[Proposition 0]{basshellerswan1964whitehead}, well before the lecture.''',r'''standard definitions, with finite generation and stabilization retained.''',refs=['bass1964stable','basshellerswan1964whitehead'],literal='correct')
item(247,'P000400-P000401','definition','Steinberg relations and the incomplete displayed presentation',r'''Two displayed relations are additivity and \([E_{ij}(a),E_{jk}(b)]=E_{ik}(ab)\). The four-factor line above them has overwritten signs and right side \(1\). The abstract group with the relations is called \(St(A)\).''',r'''In the stable Steinberg group use generators \(x_{ij}(a)\), \(i\ne j\), with \(x_{ij}(a)x_{ij}(b)=x_{ij}(a+b)\), \([x_{ij}(a),x_{kl}(b)]=1\) when \(j\ne k\) and \(i\ne l\), and \([x_{ij}(a),x_{jk}(b)]=x_{ik}(ab)\) for distinct \(i,j,k\), with a consistent commutator convention. The four factors with adjacent roots do not in general multiply to the identity: with inverse signs in the first pair they yield \(1+ab e_{ik}\).''',r'''The missing disjoint-root relation and distinct-index conditions are supplied. The overwritten first signs are left diplomatically unresolved; the erroneous identity right side is a separate mathematical defect.''','E',r'''See \citet[\S6]{vaserstein1976foundations} for the explicit stable Steinberg presentation and its low-degree role. These relations and Milnor's \(K_2\) are antecedent theory, not Kazhdan's new theorem.''',r'''the completed presentation is standard; the notes' two displayed relations alone do not specify it fully.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(247,'P000400-P000401','definition','K2 as the central kernel of the stable Steinberg group',r'''The notes write \(K_2(A)=\ker(St(A)\to E(A))\) and describe it informally as relations other than the standard ones.''',r'''For a unital associative ring, send \(x_{ij}(a)\) to \(1+ae_{ij}\). The stable Steinberg group is the universal central extension of the perfect group \(E(A)\), and \(K_2(A)\cong H_2(E(A),\mathbb Z)\). The kernel is a group of relation classes modulo Steinberg relations, not a free list of extra relators.''',r'''Centrality, universality, and the stable range are made explicit. No analogous universal-extension assertion is made for every finite-rank \(St_n(A)\).''','E',r'''The low-degree theory was available before February 1972 in Milnor's introduction; the publisher dates its edition to January 1972. The precise extension theorem was read in \citet[Theorem 6.1]{vaserstein1976foundations}; see also \citet{milnor1972introduction} for historical publication metadata.''',r'''established stable algebraic \(K_2\), independent of the later proposed higher models.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations','milnor1972introduction'],literal='correct')
item(247,'P000400','theorem','The determinant identifies K1 of a field',r'''The determinant-one subgroup of \(GL(4,\mathbb C)\) is said to be generated by elementary matrices. The final \(\mathbb C\) in \(K_1(\mathbb C)=\mathbb C\) has an uncertain upper mark, followed by ``and this holds for any field''.''',r'''For a commutative field \(F\), elementary row operations give \(SL_n(F)=E_n(F)\) for \(n\ge2\), and determinant induces \(K_1(F)\cong F^\times\). For a division ring the replacement is the abelianization of its unit group, rather than an ordinary determinant into its underlying additive group.''',r'''The multiplicative group symbol is supplied mathematically; the scan does not justify claiming that the uncertain mark is definitely a legible star.''','E',r'''This is the familiar field case of the stable Whitehead group; see \citet[Proposition 0 and the division-ring corollary after Theorem 1]{basshellerswan1964whitehead}.''',r'''established. The additive group of \(F\) is not the target.''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(247,'P000401','problem','Are universally valid elementary relations consequences of the Steinberg relations?',r'''The page explicitly labels as unsolved the question whether a relation valid in every ring follows from the Steinberg relations. It also mentions an unidentified problem in discrete group theory as a trivial case.''',r'''A precise interpretation consistent with Vaserstein's subsequent answer is \(K_2(F)=0\) for the free associative nonunital ring \(F\) on a set of generators: a stable elementary relation universal in these generators is already generated by Steinberg relations. One must specify the nonunital/reduced convention. The assertion \(K_2(R)=0\) for every ring, or even unrestricted \(K_2(\mathbb Z\langle S\rangle)=0\), is not the question and is false.''',r'''The free nonunital formulation is reconstructed from the answer on P000413 and the definition of the free-ring functor in the 1973 announcement. The cryptic discrete-group subproblem cannot be identified separately.''','B',r'''Vaserstein announces a solution in record 253. \citet[second theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations} proves vanishing on free nonunital rings in all degrees; the detailed comparison follows in \citet{vaserstein1976foundations}. Thus the 21 February ``unsolved'' label was soon obsolete. The supplied earlier-year index contains no exact occurrence.''',r'''resolved for the reconstructed free nonunital formulation. This is not a surviving open problem, and the reconstruction's quantifiers are essential.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',problem='GP-1971-20')
item(247,'P000401','construction','Embedding K0 as a Laurent K1 summand',r'''For \(B=A[t,t^{-1}]\), an automorphism of a finite projective module is embedded into an automorphism of a free module by adjoining the identity on a complement. Multiplication by \(t\) on \(P\otimes_A B\) defines \(K_0(A)\to K_1(B)\).''',r'''For \(P\oplus Q\cong A^r\), take the class of \(t\,1_{P_B}\oplus1_{Q_B}\) in \(K_1(B)\). It is independent of the complement and free identification, additive in \(P\), and defines a split injection \(K_0(A)\hookrightarrow K_1(B)\). The indeterminate is central, also when \(A\) is noncommutative.''',r'''The projective-automorphism definition explains why the construction does not require \(P\) itself to be free.''','E',r'''The construction is the map \(h\) of \citet[pp.62--63 and \S5]{basshellerswan1964whitehead}. Their regular-ring decomposition predates the talk; the summand construction and index remain meaningful more generally, with Nil terms retained.''',r'''standard part of the fundamental theorem for Laurent polynomial \(K\)-theory.''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead'],literal='correct')
item(247,'P000402','construction','Polynomial cokernels and the normalization of the Laurent index',r'''Write a Laurent invertible matrix \(L=t^{-n}L'\) with polynomial entries in \(L'\); its cokernel is said to be a finite projective \(A\)-module. The page calls this the inverse construction but does not subtract the shift contribution.''',r'''If \(g\in GL_r(A[t,t^{-1}])\), choose \(n\ge0\) with \(t^ng\in M_r(A[t])\). The Laurent index is \(\partial[g]=[\operatorname{coker}(t^ng:A[t]^r\to A[t]^r)]-nr[A]\). The cokernel is finite projective as an \(A\)-module. Increasing \(n\) by one adds \(r[A]\) to the first term, so the difference is independent of \(n\), and \(\partial h[P]=[P]\).''',r'''The subtractive term is required; record 249 supplies it explicitly. The formula is written for stable classes, not claimed to turn two nonisomorphic polynomial cokernels into isomorphic modules.''','E',r'''This is the index construction behind \citet[\S5]{basshellerswan1964whitehead}; the modern version is also given in \citet[III, \S3]{weibel2013kbook}. The following week's return to it is a clarification of an old construction.''',r'''established after normalization. Using only the cokernel class would assign a nonzero answer to the identity when one chooses \(n>0\).''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead','weibel2013kbook'],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(247,'P000401-P000402','theorem','The K1 summand in Laurent K2',r'''Beside \(K_0(A)\subset K_1(B)\), the notes assert that \(K_1(A)\) is a direct summand of \(K_2(B)\), and leave construction of the forward map as an exercise.''',r'''For a unital associative ring \(A\) and central \(t\), the Laurent fundamental theorem has a natural split summand \(K_1(A)\subset K_2(A[t,t^{-1}])\); in full generality \(K_2(B)\cong K_2(A)\oplus K_1(A)\oplus NK_2(A)\oplus NK_2(A)\), where \(NK_2(A)=\ker(K_2(A[t])\to K_2(A))\).''',r'''The full modern decomposition is supplied to prevent suppressing the two Nil terms. The notebook asserts only the indicated summand.''','B',r'''\citet{gersten1971laurent} had already proved a related regular-ring surjectivity result (received June 1970, printed October 1971), not the exact general decomposition. The general statement is among the explicit Laurent formulas of \citet[third theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations}; record 253 returns to it and cites Wagoner in the notes. The class records the checked general publication rather than attributing priority solely to this lecture.''',r'''established; omission of Nil terms is justified only under additional regularity or vanishing hypotheses.''',refs=['gersten1971laurent','vaserstein1973foundations'],literal='correct',confidence='medium')
item(247,'P000402-P000403','construction','Truncation modulo finite matrices as a boundary construction',r'''Two-sided row-and-column-finite matrices are truncated to one side. A marginal correction restricts them to finitely many diagonals parallel to the main diagonal. A lifted elementary relation has image \(1\) modulo finite matrices and is then described as a finite matrix representing \(K_1(A)\).''',r'''For two-sided finite-band matrices, compression to the nonnegative half-line is multiplicative modulo the ideal of finitely supported matrices: the defect factors through only finitely many indices at the cut. Laurent multiplication matrices lie in this algebra. Lifting a Steinberg relation through the compression produces an invertible \(1+F\) with \(F\) finitely supported, hence a stable \(K_1(A)\)-class. It is \(L-1\), not \(L\), that has finite support.''',r'''This reconstructs the algebraic Toeplitz boundary and makes explicit the finite-support defect. Independence of lifts is part of the boundary theorem; the notebook's sketches alone are not a proof.''','E',r'''The construction is an index-theoretic realization of the Laurent boundary, developed in the foundational literature and made precise in the suspension and exact-sequence treatment of \citet{vaserstein1973foundations}. For prior Laurent constructions compare \citet{basshellerswan1964whitehead,gersten1971laurent}.''',r'''standard after imposing the notebook's own bandwidth correction and reading the last matrix as identity plus a finite matrix.''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead','gersten1971laurent','vaserstein1973foundations'],literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='medium')
corr('P000400','four-factor elementary-matrix display',r'Four factors with adjacent roots are equated to \(1\); first two signs overwritten.',r'Retain sign ambiguity; with inverse signs the adjacent-root commutator is \(1+ab e_{ik}\), not \(1\).','Scan inspected. The final identity is visible; multiplication for distinct i,j,k gives the nonzero ik term.',typ='faithful_error')
corr('P000400','last field example',r'\(K_1(\mathbb C)=\mathbb C\) with unresolved upper mark',r'\(K_1(\mathbb C)=\mathbb C^\times\).','Scan inspected: upper mark remains ambiguous; determinant determines the required multiplicative group.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000402','inverse construction before the finite-matrix argument','Cokernel class alone represents the Laurent boundary.',r'Use \([\operatorname{coker}(t^ng)]-nr[A]\).','Dependence on the clearing power is immediate on the identity matrix; the normalization is explicitly supplied in the next Kirillov occurrence.')
corr('P000403','last line of the truncated elementary relation','L is over the finite-matrix ideal.',r'\(L-1\) is in the finite-matrix ideal; \(L=1+F\) represents a stable automorphism.','Scan inspected: the conclusion follows from Psi_2(L)=1, which means L-1 lies in the kernel.')
