talk(248,'1972-02-21','A. M. Gabrielov','P000403-P000405','Polarized Hodge structures and the Hodge--Riemann signs',r'''Gabrielov explicitly reports Griffiths's work on variation of Hodge structure, with pointers to Griffiths's 1970 survey and a Deligne Bourbaki report. This first installment develops the geometry of one smooth projective variety: Hodge decomposition, Lefschetz decomposition, the primitive polarization, and the abstract linear-algebraic definition. These are prior results presented as preparation for the family theory of record 251. A sign inconsistency in the positive-definiteness formula is visible in the scan.''')
item(248,'P000403','definition','An integral polarization and Hodge decomposition',r'''For a nonsingular complex projective \(X\) of dimension \(d\), the notes introduce \(H^n(X,\mathbb Z)\), its complexification, and a closed integral \((1,1)\)-form \(\eta\), called a polarization. They write \(H^n(X,\mathbb C)=\bigoplus_{p+q=n}H^{p,q}\).''',r'''For smooth projective \(X/\mathbb C\), take \(\eta=c_1(L)\) for an ample line bundle, represented by a K\"ahler form. Then \(H^n(X,\mathbb C)=\bigoplus H^{p,q}(X)\), with \(\overline{H^{p,q}}=H^{q,p}\). The integral lattice for a pure integral Hodge structure is \(H^n(X,\mathbb Z)/\mathrm{torsion}\); complexification kills torsion.''',r'''An arbitrary integral closed \((1,1)\)-class need not be positive. Ampleness/K\"ahler positivity and the torsion-free lattice are the missing qualifications.''','E',r'''These are the prerequisites of the already published survey \citet[\S2]{griffiths1970periods} and the pure-Hodge definitions of \citet[\S2.1]{deligne1971hodge}. Griffiths's text derives from a 1968 address and appeared in March 1970.''',r'''established classical Hodge theory under the stated compactness and positivity hypotheses.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods','deligne1971hodge'],area='Hodge theory')
item(248,'P000404','theorem','Hard Lefschetz, primitive cohomology, and orthogonal decomposition',r'''The operator \(\eta\) raises bidegree by \((1,1)\). Primitive cohomology is the kernel of a high power of \(\eta\), and the notes decompose cohomology into powers of \(\eta\) times primitive pieces.''',r'''Let \(L=\eta\smile-\). For \(0\le n\le d\), \(L^{d-n}:H^n(X,\mathbb Q)\to H^{2d-n}(X,\mathbb Q)\) is an isomorphism. Put \(P^n=\ker(L^{d-n+1}:H^n\to H^{2d-n+2})\). Then \(H^n=\bigoplus_{j\ge0}L^jP^{n-2j}\), and this decomposition respects Hodge types. With a K\"ahler metric the associated primitive Lefschetz components are orthogonal for the Hodge inner product.''',r'''The degree range, coefficient field, and which inner product supplies orthogonality are stated explicitly; the formula for primitive degree is not extended beyond its intended range without reinterpretation.''','E',r'''\citet[\S2, pp.234--235]{griffiths1970periods} explicitly passes to primitive cohomology and gives the Lefschetz decomposition; this is classical input to the variation theory.''',r'''established, not an instance of the Hodge conjecture.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Hodge theory')
item(248,'P000404-P000405','theorem','Hodge--Riemann bilinear relations and a sign inconsistency',r'''The notes use \(\Psi(x,y)=(-1)^{n(n+1)/2}\int_X\eta^{d-n}\wedge x\wedge y\). They state \(i^{p-q}\Psi(x,\bar x)>0\) and later require \(\Psi(x,C\bar x)>0\), where \(C|_{H^{p,q}}=i^{p-q}\).''',r'''On primitive \(H^{p,q}\), \(p+q=n\le d\), let \(Q(x,y)=(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}\int_Xx\wedge y\wedge\eta^{d-n}\). Then \(Q(y,x)=(-1)^nQ(x,y)\), \(Q(H^{p,q},H^{r,s})=0\) unless \((r,s)=(q,p)\), and \(i^{p-q}Q(x,\bar x)>0\) for \(x\ne0\). With the notebook's \(\Psi=(-1)^nQ\), positivity is instead \(i^{q-p}\Psi(x,\bar x)>0\), equivalently \(\Psi(x,C\bar x)>0\).''',r'''Two consistent conventions are displayed rather than altering one sign without explanation. The source's first and last positivity assertions differ in odd weight.''','E',r'''Compare the polarization conventions in \citet[\S2 and equations (9.8)--(9.9)]{griffiths1970periods} and \citet[Definition 2.1.16]{deligne1971hodge}. The scan confirms both the \(n(n+1)/2\) exponent and \(i^{p-q}\) in the first inequality.''',r'''the bilinear relations are established; the literal combination of signs in the notes fails already in weight one.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods','deligne1971hodge'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Hodge theory')
item(248,'P000405','definition','Abstract polarized Hodge structures and the Weil operator',r'''The end replaces geometric cohomology by a finite-dimensional vector space with a real form, a \((p,q)\)-decomposition, the operator \(C\), and a bilinear form satisfying symmetry and positivity conditions.''',r'''A real Hodge structure of weight \(n\) is a finite-dimensional real vector space \(V\) with \(V_\mathbb C=\bigoplus_{p+q=n}V^{p,q}\), \(\overline{V^{p,q}}=V^{q,p}\). A polarization additionally has the type orthogonality and signed positivity stated in the preceding item. The Weil operator is real on \(V\), acts by \(i^{p-q}\) on \(V^{p,q}\), and satisfies \(C^2=(-1)^n\). An integral structure adds a lattice; invariance under \(C\) alone does not replace full Hodge-type orthogonality.''',r'''The distinction between a real structure and an integral lattice is made explicit. This is a definition and does not assert that every abstract polarized Hodge structure comes from a smooth projective variety.''','E',r'''See \citet[\S2.1, especially Definition 2.1.16]{deligne1971hodge} and \citet[\S9]{griffiths1970periods}. Both were published before Gabrielov's report.''',r'''standard linear-algebraic structure used in variations and period domains.''',refs=['deligne1971hodge','griffiths1970periods'],area='Hodge theory')
corr('P000404','third bilinear relation and later Weil-operator condition',r'With \(\Psi=(-1)^{n(n+1)/2}\int\), the notes write \(i^{p-q}\Psi(x,\bar x)>0\).',r'Keep Psi and replace by \(i^{q-p}\Psi(x,\bar x)>0\), or change the initial exponent to \(n(n-1)/2\).','Scan inspected: both source exponents are clear. The later Psi(x,C bar x)>0 agrees with the first correction; the two conventions differ by (-1)^n.')
save()

talk(249,'1972-02-28','A. A. Kirillov','P000405-P000407','The normalized Laurent index and polynomial Nil groups',r'''This lecture revisits the preceding Laurent construction, explicitly noting that one must subtract the excess introduced by clearing negative powers. Gelfand repeats the projective-module map, and the final part states the fundamental exact sequence, the Nil summand, and Higman linearization. The catalogue date is preserved in metadata; the handwritten heading is clearly \(27/\mathrm{II}\), not \(28/\mathrm{II}\). The talk begins near the bottom of P000405 and ends at the divider before Morava on P000407.''')
item(249,'P000405-P000406','construction','The shift-independent Laurent index revisited',r'''Kirillov writes \(M=t^{-k}M_1=t^{-\ell}M_2\), observes that polynomial multiplication has zero kernel, and explicitly adds ``one must subtract the excess''. The same symbol \(k\) is also used for matrix size.''',r'''For \(g\in GL_r(A[t,t^{-1}])\) and \(N\) large enough, define \(\partial[g]=[\operatorname{coker}(t^Ng)]-Nr[A]\). The first cokernel is finite projective over \(A\). Multiplying by another \(t\) changes its class by \(r[A]\), proving independence of \(N\). Elementary changes preserve the resulting stable index; arbitrary Laurent elementary changes need not preserve a chosen polynomial cokernel before normalization.''',r'''Separate symbols \(r,N\) prevent confusing size with denominator-clearing order. The positive-cokernel convention is chosen so that multiplication by \(t\) has index \(+[A]\); the analytic Fredholm convention would have the opposite sign.''','E',r'''This clarifies occurrence 1971-247.7. The index construction was already in \citet[\S5]{basshellerswan1964whitehead}; see \citet[III, \S3]{weibel2013kbook} for the general form.''',r'''established; the new lecture's significance is the recorded repair of the missing normalization.''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead','weibel2013kbook'])
item(249,'P000406','construction','The projective-module splitting and Gelfand’s repetition',r'''For \(N\oplus M=A^r\), the automorphism \(t\) on \(N_B\) and \(1\) on \(M_B\) gives a free \(B\)-matrix. The notes compute \(\operatorname{coker}(t:N[t]\to N[t])=N\). Gelfand repeats the diagram, whose displayed scalar-extended module is erroneously labelled \(A^r\).''',r'''The matrix for \(t1_{N_B}\oplus1_{M_B}\) is already polynomial after choosing an \(A\)-linear free identification \(N\oplus M\cong A^r\). Its polynomial cokernel is \(N\), so \(\partial h=1\) on \(K_0(A)\). Scalar extension yields \(N_B\oplus M_B\cong B^r\), not \(A^r\).''',r'''This verifies one side of the splitting and does not assert that the two maps are inverses on all of \(K_1(B)\), which contains the other summands.''','E',r'''The map and its left inverse are those of \citet[pp.62--63 and \S5]{basshellerswan1964whitehead}. The two recorded explanations are part of the same construction, not two separate discoveries.''',r'''established; the module label requires correction.''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead'],literal='false as written',match='partial',speaker='A. A. Kirillov; intervention by I. M. Gelfand')
item(249,'P000407','theorem','The Laurent exact sequence and polynomial Nil summands',r'''The notes display \(0\to K_1(A)\to K_1(A[t])\oplus K_1(A[t^{-1}])\to K_1(A[t,t^{-1}])\to K_0(A)\to0\), and \(K_1(A[t])=K_1(A)\oplus\operatorname{Nil}A\). They say that Nil vanishes for regular rings.''',r'''For a unital ring, set \(NK_1(A)=\ker(K_1(A[t])\xrightarrow{t\mapsto0}K_1(A))\). Evaluation splits the polynomial group. The displayed Laurent sequence, with opposite signs on the two constant inclusions, is exact and its right boundary is split by \(h\). Thus \(K_1(A[t,t^{-1}])\cong K_1(A)\oplus K_0(A)\oplus NK_1(A)^{\oplus2}\). If \(A\) is left regular noetherian (every finite left module has finite projective dimension), then \(NK_1(A)=0\).''',r'''The definition of regularity and the two copies of the Nil group are supplied. ``Functions on the suspension'' on P000405 is an index-theoretic analogy: algebraically the Laurent extension corresponds to product with the multiplicative group, not literally to a topological suspension.''','E',r'''\citet[Theorems 1--2]{basshellerswan1964whitehead} prove the regular version; \citet[III, Theorem 3.6 and \S3]{weibel2013kbook} gives the general formulation including Nil groups. Earlier item 1968-171.8 concerns related polynomial matrices, but no identical indexed problem was located.''',r'''established, with regularity essential for deleting Nil terms.''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead','weibel2013kbook'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(249,'P000407','method','Higman linearization and nilpotent matrices',r'''After removing the constant \(K_1\)-part, the notes write \(M=1+tN(t)\). Enlarging the matrix reduces higher powers to first order, so \(N\) becomes constant, and polynomial invertibility forces \(N^k=0\).''',r'''A polynomial matrix invertible over \(A[t]\) with constant term \(I\) becomes, after stabilization and elementary equivalence, \(I-tN\) for a finite nilpotent matrix \(N\) over \(A\). Conversely \(I-tN\) is polynomially invertible exactly when \(N\) is nilpotent, with inverse the finite geometric series. Nilpotent endomorphisms supply generators and relations for the Nil group; their raw set of matrices is not itself a group.''',r'''The sign of \(N\) is conventional. Stable equivalence, rather than equality of the original matrices, is the content of the linearization.''','E',r'''This is the classical Higman trick, used in \citet[Proposition 2.1 and its proof]{basshellerswan1964whitehead} and explained in \citet[III, \S3]{weibel2013kbook}.''',r'''established and still a useful computational model of polynomial Nil phenomena.''',refs=['basshellerswan1964whitehead','weibel2013kbook'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
corr('P000405','Kirillov date heading','Catalogue metadata: 28 February 1972.','Notebook heading: 27/II. Catalogue date retained with this qualification.','Scan inspected: the second digit has the angular form of 7. No silent harmonization to the expected Monday date.',typ='provenance')
