talk(250,'1972-02-28','Jack Morava','P000407-P000408','Quillen’s plus construction and algebraic K-theory',r'''Morava explains the construction that kills a perfect normal subgroup while preserving homology, applies it to the discrete stable general linear group, and notes that degree zero has to be added separately. The talk compares this theory with Milnor's \(K_2\), Volodin's construction, and topological \(K\)-theory. It closes with Novikov's broad proposal for a Chern character. This is a report on Quillen's already circulating work, not the invention of the plus construction by Morava.''')
item(250,'P000407','theorem','Killing a perfect normal subgroup by the plus construction',r'''For \(H\triangleleft\pi_1(X)\), \(H=[H,H]\), the notes attach two- and three-cells, obtain \(\pi_1(X_H)=\pi_1(X)/H\), and preserve integral homology. They claim uniqueness up to homotopy and functoriality.''',r'''For a connected CW complex \(X\) and a perfect normal subgroup \(H\), there is a plus construction \(X\to X_H^+\) obtained by attaching cells in dimensions 2 and 3, with fundamental group quotient \(\pi_1(X)/H\). The map is acyclic: it induces homology isomorphisms with every local coefficient module over that quotient. The resulting map under \(X\) is unique up to homotopy. Ordinary untwisted homology alone is not the full characterization.''',r'''CW hypotheses, local coefficients, and uniqueness of the map under \(X\) are supplied. The phrase ``homology ring'' is replaced by homology groups unless an additional multiplication is given.''','E',r'''Quillen's construction circulated before the lecture; \citet[IV, Theorem 1.5 and Lemma 1.6]{weibel2013kbook} dates the announcement to 1969 and gives the acyclic-map formulation. Vaserstein's 1973 announcement cites Quillen's 1970 ICM account as an existing definition.''',r'''established. The local-coefficient condition is essential to the stated uniqueness formulation.''',refs=['weibel2013kbook','vaserstein1973foundations'],match='partial')
item(250,'P000408','definition','The discrete GL plus model and the missing degree zero',r'''Morava stresses that \(GL(R)\) is discrete, kills its perfect elementary subgroup, and defines \(K_i(R)=\pi_i(BGL(R)^+)\). The crossed-out line about \(K_0\) is replaced by a note that the definition must change because the space is connected.''',r'''For a unital associative ring \(R\), define \(K_i(R)=\pi_i(BGL(R)^+)\) for \(i\ge1\), where plus kills the stable elementary subgroup. A representing space for connective algebraic \(K\)-theory is \(K_0(R)\times BGL(R)^+\), with the group of components \(K_0(R)\). Its fundamental group is \(GL(R)/E(R)\).''',r'''The subscript range and component factor are explicitly restored. The source itself notices the degree-zero issue, so this is not counted as an uncorrected false theorem.''','E',r'''This is Quillen's plus definition as summarized in \citet[IV, \S1]{weibel2013kbook}; the comparison with the earlier low groups is included in \citet[first theorem]{vaserstein1973foundations}.''',r'''standard connective algebraic \(K\)-theory; connectedness of \(BGL^+\) remains compatible with nonzero \(K_0\).''',refs=['weibel2013kbook','vaserstein1973foundations'],literal='correct')
item(250,'P000408','theorem','Agreement of Quillen K2 with the classical field group',r'''The notes attribute to Quillen the equality \(K_2^{\mathrm{Quillen}}(R)=k_2^{\mathrm{Milnor}}(R)\) for a field.''',r'''For a field \(F\), Quillen's \(\pi_2BGL(F)^+\) agrees with the classical Steinberg-kernel \(K_2(F)\), and Matsumoto's theorem identifies it with \((F^\times\otimes_{\mathbb Z}F^\times)/\langle a\otimes(1-a):a\ne0,1\rangle\). Agreement with the stable Steinberg-kernel definition holds for unital rings as well; the symbolic presentation stated here is the field case.''',r'''The modern distinction between Milnor's original ring \(K_2\) and the Milnor symbol group of a field is made explicit; no assertion is extended to equality of higher Milnor and Quillen groups.''','E',r'''The low-degree identification belongs to the original plus theory; see \citet[IV, \S1]{weibel2013kbook} and the explicit \(K_2\)-identification in \citet[first theorem]{vaserstein1973foundations}.''',r'''established in degree two. The field qualification does not justify replacing all higher algebraic \(K\)-groups by Milnor symbols.''',refs=['weibel2013kbook','vaserstein1973foundations'],literal='correct',match='exact')
item(250,'P000408','remark','Discrete and topological general linear groups give different theories',r'''After insisting on discreteness, Morava says that a version for topological groups gives the usual \(K\)-functor for \(\mathbb R\) and \(\mathbb C\), and mentions Volodin's alternative definition.''',r'''Using \(GL_n(\mathbb C)\) or \(GL_n(\mathbb R)\) with their analytic topologies and group completion leads to the representing spaces \(\mathbb Z\times BU\) and \(\mathbb Z\times BO\) for connective complex and real topological \(K\)-theory. Using the same fields as discrete rings yields algebraic \(K\)-theory. The continuous inclusion from the discrete group induces a comparison map, not an equality of the theories.''',r'''The precise spaces clarify the dependence on topology. Periodic topological \(K\)-theory additionally inverts Bott periodicity; it is not obtained by changing only the notation for \(K_0\).''','E',r'''The distinction is built into the classical classifying-space approach and is reviewed alongside algebraic models in \citet[IV, \S1]{weibel2013kbook}. Volodin's original construction was published in 1971, received July 1970 \citep{volodin1971extraordinary}.''',r'''established comparison of different functors; their values must not be substituted for one another.''',refs=['weibel2013kbook','volodin1971extraordinary'],match='partial')
item(250,'P000408','construction','Cohomology on spaces from the K-theory representing space',r'''A displayed definition reads \(K_i(R)(Y)=[S^i\wedge Y;X_{E(R)}]\), with a bracket labelled suspension.''',r'''For a pointed CW complex \(Y\), stable algebraic \(K\)-cohomology is defined by the connective \(K(R)\)-spectrum. Its reduced groups satisfy \(\widetilde K(R)^{-i}(Y)=[\Sigma^iY,\Omega^\infty K(R)]_*\) for \(i\ge0\), with the appropriate base component when \(\Sigma^iY\) is connected. The full zeroth space includes \(K_0(R)\). An arbitrary pointed space without compatible deloopings does not by itself define a generalized cohomology theory in every degree.''',r'''Based homotopy classes, degree-sign convention, component information, and the spectrum structure are supplied. The page is a motivation for a cohomology theory, not a complete construction of its deloopings.''','B',r'''The categorical spectrum construction is developed in \citet[\S\S1--4]{segal1974categories}, the subject of Morava's next talk (record 255); the paper was received in August 1972 and published in 1974.''',r'''standard representability once the infinite-loop-space structure is included.''',refs=['segal1974categories'],match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(250,'P000408','problem','Novikov’s Chern-character programme',r'''The closing sentence attributes to Novikov the problem of defining a Chern character in algebraic \(K\)-theory and says that here it reduces to computing \(H_*(BGL(R)^+;\mathbb Q)\).''',r'''The source does not specify the rings, target theory, grading, naturality requirements, or normalization of a desired character. Computing the rational homology of the plus space is meaningful and equals rational group homology of \(GL(R)\), but it does not uniquely specify a Chern-character map to a prescribed cohomology or homology theory.''',r'''No later regulator, Dennis trace, cyclic-homology character, or motivic character is selected as the unique intended solution. Such choices answer distinct precise questions.''','F',r'''Searches for Novikov's algebraic Chern character, the 1972 seminar, Morava, and rational homology gave broad related programmes rather than an exact uniquely specified problem. The homology preservation itself is part of the plus construction \citep[IV, \S1]{weibel2013kbook}.''',r'''the programme is historically recognizable but too broad for a definite problem-index entry or a claim of present-day openness.''',refs=['weibel2013kbook'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',speaker='S. P. Novikov, as reported by Jack Morava')
save()

talk(251,'1972-02-28','A. M. Gabrielov','P000409-P000410','The holomorphic Hodge filtration and Gauss–Manin connection',r'''Gabrielov continues the exposition of variation of Hodge structure. The notes pass from the Hodge decomposition on a fibre to the relative de Rham complex, its spectral sequence, holomorphic subbundles, and the flat Gauss--Manin connection. The distinction between inverse-image sheaves and module pullback is explicitly emphasized. A strict inequality in the initial filtration and an increasing exact sequence later conflict with the intended decreasing-filtration convention; they are recorded as such rather than silently propagated.''')
item(251,'P000409','theorem','Holomorphic variation of the Hodge filtration',r'''The initial formula is \(F^p=\bigoplus_{p'>p}H^{p',q}\), and the notes emphasize that the filtration ``moves holomorphically''. They contrast it with the nonholomorphic behaviour of individual \((p,q)\)-pieces.''',r'''For a smooth projective morphism \(f:Y\to S\) of smooth complex varieties, the vector bundle \(\mathcal H^n=(R^nf_*\mathbb C)\otimes\mathcal O_S\) has holomorphic subbundles \(F^p\mathcal H^n\) whose fibre is \(\bigoplus_{r\ge p}H^{r,n-r}(Y_s)\). The Hodge decomposition gives a smooth splitting, generally not a holomorphic splitting into all \(H^{p,q}\).''',r'''The modern decreasing convention uses \(r\ge p\), not \(r>p\). A globally shifted convention could be chosen, but it would require changing all later indices consistently.''','E',r'''The exact holomorphic-filtration statement is in \citet[\S2(b)]{griffiths1970periods}, published before the lecture. That survey uses an increasing reindexing, which helps explain but does not remove the notebook's mixed conventions.''',r'''established variation-of-Hodge-structure theory; the literal index requires repair.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Hodge theory')
item(251,'P000409','construction','Smooth families and the cohomological local system',r'''The family is locally projective over a nonsingular base. ``Smooth'' is glossed as local triviality in the analytic sense, followed by a locally trivial holomorphic bundle with fibre \(H^n(Y_s,\mathbb C)\).''',r'''A smooth proper map of complex manifolds is locally a differentiable fibre bundle, and \(R^nf_*\mathbb C\) is a finite-rank local system. Its tensor product with \(\mathcal O_S\) is a holomorphic flat vector bundle. A holomorphic submersion is locally a projection near each point of the total space; this does not imply that the entire family is holomorphically a product over a neighbourhood in the base.''',r'''The two senses of local triviality are separated. The notebook may mean the local submersion normal form; an assertion of isotriviality of all fibres is not adopted.''','E',r'''\citet[\S1 and \S2(a)]{griffiths1970periods} states the smooth proper projective setting and identifies the flat bundle with the cohomological local system.''',r'''established. Varying complex structures are fully compatible with differentiable local triviality.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],match='partial',area='Hodge theory')
item(251,'P000409','construction','Inverse-image coefficients and the relative de Rham resolution',r'''The source distinguishes the set-theoretic inverse-image sheaf \(f^\circ\mathcal O_S\) from the tensor pullback \(f^*\mathcal O_S=\mathcal O_Y\), and writes \(0\to f^\circ\mathcal O_S\to\mathcal O_Y\to\Omega^1_{Y/S}\to\cdots\).''',r'''For a holomorphic submersion, let \(f^{-1}\mathcal O_S\) denote the inverse-image sheaf of rings and \(f^*\mathcal O_S=f^{-1}\mathcal O_S\otimes_{f^{-1}\mathcal O_S}\mathcal O_Y\cong\mathcal O_Y\). Define \(\Omega^1_{Y/S}=\Omega_Y^1/\operatorname{im}(f^*\Omega_S^1)\) and exterior powers. The relative holomorphic Poincar\'e lemma makes \(\Omega^\bullet_{Y/S}\) a resolution of \(f^{-1}\mathcal O_S\) on the analytic site.''',r'''The two pullback symbols are modernized without erasing the notebook's explicit distinction. The complex is a resolution in the analytic sheaf setting, not an exact sequence of global sections.''','E',r'''This is the relative de Rham construction used in \citet[\S2(b), Grothendieck's formulation]{griffiths1970periods}; it is already part of the theory being reported.''',r'''standard, with the analytic topology and local nature of exactness stated.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct',area='Hodge theory')
item(251,'P000410','theorem','Degeneration of the relative Hodge-to-de Rham spectral sequence',r'''The page calls the sequence Leray and writes \(E_1^{p,q}=R^qf_*\Omega^p_{Y/S}\), says all differentials vanish, and identifies the resulting filtration by holomorphic subbundles. It also displays \(\mathcal F^n=R^nf_*\Omega^0\) and \(0\to\mathcal F^{p-1}\to\mathcal F^p\to E_\infty^{p,n-p}\to0\).''',r'''For a smooth proper projective complex family, \(E_1^{p,q}=R^qf_*\Omega^p_{Y/S}\Rightarrow\mathbb R^{p+q}f_*\Omega^\bullet_{Y/S}\) degenerates at \(E_1\). The terms are locally free and compatible with base change. In decreasing indexing, \(0\to F^{p+1}\mathcal H^n\to F^p\mathcal H^n\to R^{n-p}f_*\Omega^p_{Y/S}\to0\), and \(F^n\mathcal H^n=f_*\Omega^n_{Y/S}\).''',r'''The sequence is the relative Hodge-to-de Rham hypercohomology spectral sequence. The corrected indices follow the convention fixed in item 1971-251.1; the source's formulas combine incompatible conventions.''','E',r'''The degeneration and construction of the Hodge filtration are the prior Hodge-theoretic input described in \citet[\S2(b)]{griffiths1970periods}. The scan confirms the reversed direct-image and differential-form indices.''',r'''established for the stated family; the displayed indices in the notes are false under their own initial decreasing convention.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Hodge theory')
item(251,'P000410','construction','The Gauss–Manin connection',r'''The last lines identify \(R^nf_*f^\circ\mathcal O_S\) with \(R^nf_*\mathbb C\otimes\mathcal O_S\), mark locally constant cohomology classes as sections, and display a connection into \(\Omega_S^1\otimes\mathcal H^n\).''',r'''The cohomological local system defines the flat holomorphic connection \(\nabla:\mathcal H^n\to\Omega_S^1\otimes\mathcal H^n\), characterized by having \(R^nf_*\mathbb C\) as horizontal sections. In the full variation theorem it satisfies Griffiths transversality \(\nabla F^p\subseteq\Omega_S^1\otimes F^{p-1}\).''',r'''Flatness follows from the local-system construction. The transversality inclusion is supplied as context from the cited theorem; it is not claimed to be written on these two notebook pages.''','E',r'''\citet[\S2(a)--(c), equation (2.1)]{griffiths1970periods} provides the flat connection and infinitesimal period relation. Both predate the lecture.''',r'''standard Gauss--Manin and Hodge-filtration structure; the notebook's final formula is its beginning, not a complete proof.''',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Hodge theory')
corr('P000409','initial Hodge filtration',r'\(F^p=\bigoplus_{p\prime>p}H^{p\prime,q}\)',r'In the decreasing convention use \(p\prime\ge p\).','Scan inspected: the strict sign is present. A shifted convention would require corresponding changes to all subsequent indices.')
corr('P000409','smoothness gloss','Smooth means local triviality in the analytic sense.','Local projection near each point is valid; local triviality of the entire family is differentiable, not generally holomorphic.','Scan inspected. The wording does not distinguish the two scopes of local triviality.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000410','last step and exact sequence of the filtration',r'\(F^n=R^nf_*\Omega^0\); \(0\to F^{p-1}\to F^p\to E_\infty^{p,n-p}\to0\)',r'\(F^n=f_*\Omega^n\); \(0\to F^{p+1}\to F^p\to R^{n-p}f_*\Omega^p\to0\).','Scan inspected. This is the consistent decreasing filtration associated with the displayed E_1^{p,q}=R^q f_*Omega^p.')
save()

