talk(252,'1972-03-06','M. Sh. Birman','P000411-P000412','Scattering channels, trace ideals, and the spectral shift',r'''Birman starts with the Lax--Phillips exterior-wave picture, then gives the abstract definition of wave operators and asks for completeness. The principal results reported are the trace-class theorem, its invariance and resolvent-comparison extensions, the Birman--Krein determinant formula, and localized trace-ideal criteria for one or two Hilbert spaces. All have antecedents before this lecture. The compressed opening must distinguish conserved total energy from decay of a compressed or spatially localized evolution; the closing two-space statement must replace equality of domains in different spaces by a condition on the identification operator.''')
item(252,'P000411','construction','Exterior waves and the Lax–Phillips interaction space',r'''For the exterior Dirichlet wave equation \(u_{tt}=\Delta u\), the notes assume Huygens' principle and write the conserved energy \(\int_\Omega(|\nabla u|^2+|u_t|^2)\,dx\). Incoming and outgoing spaces \(K_-,K_+\) leave a complement \(K_0\), described as exponentially decaying solutions concentrated near the boundary.''',r'''For a smooth compact obstacle in odd-dimensional Euclidean space of dimension at least three, the Dirichlet wave group acts unitarily on its finite-energy space. Suitable incoming and outgoing subspaces \(D_-,D_+\) are orthogonal, and the interaction space is \(\mathcal K=\mathcal H\ominus(D_+\oplus D_-)\). The compressed evolution on \(\mathcal K\) is a contraction semigroup. Resonant states and local-energy decay belong to this reduced description. A nonzero finite-energy solution cannot decay in its full energy norm, which is conserved; uniform exponential local decay requires further geometric and analytic hypotheses not stated here.''',r'''The exterior region, dimension, boundary regularity, and distinction between total and local or compressed energy are supplied. The page does not justify an exponential-decay theorem for every obstacle or every vector of \(K_0\).''','E',r'''The cited framework was already developed in \citet[Chapters II--IV]{laxphillips1967scattering}; the precise exterior-wave subject of the next work is \citet{laxphillips1969decaying}. The publisher's contents and article abstract were checked, but this compressed source statement was not matched to a theorem with the omitted geometric hypotheses.''',r'''the framework is established; the notebook's unrestricted exponential-decay wording remains too strong to use as a theorem.''',refs=['laxphillips1967scattering','laxphillips1969decaying'],literal='false as written',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000411','remark','Complex poles and the continued resolvent',r'''The list of keywords includes characteristic functions, the second sheet of the resolvent, the scattering matrix, and complex poles of a self-adjoint operator; Gohberg--Krein and Lax--Phillips are mentioned.''',r'''For self-adjoint \(H\), the operator-valued resolvent \((H-z)^{-1}\) is analytic for \(\operatorname{Im}z\ne0\). Nonreal scattering poles concern a meromorphic continuation of suitably localized matrix elements or resolvents, or the spectrum of a non-self-adjoint compressed generator. The existence, domain, and sheets of continuation depend on the scattering problem; the phrase cannot mean nonreal eigenvalues of \(H\) itself.''',r'''This distinguishes the objects in the keyword list without selecting a unique analytic continuation or a particular Gohberg--Krein theorem that the notes do not identify.''','E',r'''The exterior-wave resonances treated by \citet{laxphillips1969decaying} supply the identifiable prior setting. The bare phrase about complex poles has only a partial match to that theory, not the status of a separate spectral theorem.''',r'''standard resonance terminology after the continued or compressed object has been specified.''',refs=['laxphillips1969decaying'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000411','definition','Wave operators and the question of asymptotic completeness',r'''Asymptotic closeness means \(\|e^{-itH_0}f-e^{-itH}g_\pm\|\to0\). The map \(f\mapsto g_\pm=U_\pm f\) is a partial isometry intertwining \(H_0\) and \(H\). The explicit question asks when its range exhausts the absolutely continuous subspace of \(H\).''',r'''For self-adjoint operators on one Hilbert space, define \(W_\pm(H,H_0)=\operatorname{s-lim}_{t\to\pm\infty}e^{itH}e^{-itH_0}P_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)\) when the limits exist. Their initial space is \(\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)\), their ranges lie in \(\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H)\), and they intertwine the corresponding spectral calculi. Completeness means \(\operatorname{Ran}W_\pm=\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H)\). Existence alone does not imply completeness.''',r'''The absolutely continuous projection and strong operator topology are restored. The broad request is followed in the same lecture by sufficient criteria; it is not treated as one unspecified modern open problem.''','E',r'''The formulation and trace-class completeness result appear already in \citet[Theorem 1]{kato1957trace}; the two-space framework is explicit in \citet[\S1]{belopolskiiBirman1968wave}.''',r'''standard definitions and a family of precise completeness theorems. No universal criterion is claimed by the notebook.''',refs=['kato1957trace','belopolskiiBirman1968wave'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000411-P000412','definition','Orthogonal channels and multichannel completeness',r'''The notes introduce \(H_0^{(1)},\ldots,H_0^{(n)}\), requiring the ranges of the channel maps \(U_+^{(i)}\) to be mutually orthogonal and to generate the absolutely continuous part of \(H\).''',r'''Let \(H_j\) be self-adjoint channel Hamiltonians on \(\mathcal H_j\), let \(J_j:\mathcal H_j\to\mathcal H\) be bounded identifications, and suppose the channel wave operators \(W_\pm^{(j)}=\operatorname{s-lim}_{t\to\pm\infty}e^{itH}J_je^{-itH_j}P_j\) exist as isometries on their designated channel spaces \(P_j\mathcal H_j\). Orthogonality and completeness mean \(\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H)=\bigoplus_j\operatorname{Ran}W_\pm^{(j)}\). The channel spaces and identifications are part of the hypotheses.''',r'''The direct-sum condition formalizes the definition in the notes; it does not prove completeness for an arbitrary collection of Hamiltonians or interactions.''','E',r'''This is the established multichannel language of the scattering theory surveyed by the lecture. The definition of two-space wave maps, used for each channel, is in \citet[\S1]{belopolskiiBirman1968wave}; the notes themselves do not state a general many-body completeness theorem.''',r'''standard definition; actual completeness depends on the particular system and its channels.''',refs=['belopolskiiBirman1968wave'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000412','remark','Faddeev’s three-particle problem and the Lax–Phillips scheme',r'''A single sentence says that Faddeev placed the three-particle problem in the Lax--Phillips scheme.''',r'''No interaction potentials, decay assumptions, threshold exclusions, channel decomposition, or map into a Lax--Phillips representation are specified. Thus the line identifies a subject and an attribution but does not determine an exact equivalence or completeness theorem.''',r'''Faddeev's three-particle scattering theory is a firm historical lead. The additional assertion about a particular embedding into the Lax--Phillips scheme is left unresolved rather than replaced by a modern general many-body theorem.''','F',r'''The primary publication \citet{faddeev1963three} is verified as a 1963 monograph on the stated three-particle problem. Searches combining Faddeev, three particles, and Lax--Phillips did not locate the exact asserted embedding in a source that could be read here.''',r'''the broad attribution is recognizable; the specific formulation and its fate cannot be determined from this sentence.''',refs=['faddeev1963three'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000412','theorem','Kato’s trace-class perturbation theorem',r'''The line ``Kato (1957)'' is followed by \(H-H_0\) nuclear.''',r'''Let \(H_0\) be self-adjoint and let \(V\) be a bounded self-adjoint trace-class operator. Then \(H=H_0+V\), on \(D(H_0)\), is self-adjoint and both wave operators \(W_\pm(H,H_0)\) exist and are complete. They implement unitary equivalence of the absolutely continuous parts, without requiring the whole spectra to be absolutely continuous.''',r'''Boundedness, the common operator domain, and the restriction to the absolutely continuous parts are supplied. Nuclear means the Schatten trace ideal \(\mathfrak S_1\) in this Hilbert-space setting.''','E',r'''This is exactly \citet[Theorem 1, pp.260--261]{kato1957trace}, published in 1957. The paper itself acknowledges Rosenblum's contemporaneous related result; the lecture reports the theorem under Kato's name.''',r'''established as the Kato--Rosenblum trace-class theorem.''',refs=['kato1957trace'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000412','theorem','Birman’s invariance principle and resolvent comparability',r'''The next line, dated 1962 and attributed to Birman, replaces \(H-H_0\) by \(\varphi(H)-\varphi(H_0)\) in the nuclear condition.''',r'''A precise sufficient version is: if \(H,H_0\) are self-adjoint and \((H-z)^{-1}-(H_0-z)^{-1}\in\mathfrak S_1\) for one nonreal \(z\), then the ordinary wave operators exist and are complete. More general invariance principles permit suitable monotone changes of spectral variable with additional regularity and localization hypotheses. Trace-class difference for an arbitrary function \(\varphi\) cannot suffice: a constant \(\varphi\) makes it zero for every pair.''',r'''The verified resolvent theorem replaces the underqualified function shorthand. The constant-function observation shows why the missing hypotheses are substantive; no maximal class of admissible functions is inferred.''','E',r'''\citet[Theorem 2 and the following condition \(B_n\), pp.476--477]{birmanKrein1962wave} prove the stated resolvent-comparison result and describe further semibounded inverse-power conditions.''',r'''established under the specified comparison hypothesis. The unrestricted functional-calculus formulation is false.''',refs=['birmanKrein1962wave'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000412','construction','The scattering operator and its spectral fibres',r'''The source defines \(S=U_+^*U_-\) and calls \(S(\lambda)\) the matrix in the spectral decomposition of \(H_0\).''',r'''When both wave operators exist and are complete, \(S=W_+^*W_-\) is a unitary operator on \(\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)\). It commutes with the spectral measure of \(H_0\), hence in a direct-integral representation it acts by a measurable family of unitaries \(S(\lambda)\), defined almost everywhere. These fibres may act on infinite-dimensional multiplicity spaces; the word matrix does not imply finite dimension.''',r'''Completeness, the almost-everywhere convention, and possible infinite spectral multiplicity are added.''','E',r'''This is the construction used explicitly in \citet[\S4]{birmanKrein1962wave} and \citet[\S6]{belopolskiiBirman1968wave}.''',r'''standard. Its determinant requires the extra trace-ideal conclusion in the following item.''',refs=['birmanKrein1962wave','belopolskiiBirman1968wave'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000412','theorem','The spectral shift and Birman–Krein determinant formula',r'''The displayed formulas are \(S(\lambda)-I\) nuclear, \(\det S(\lambda)=e^{-2\pi i\xi(\lambda)}\), and \(\operatorname{Tr}(\varphi(H)-\varphi(H_0))=\int\varphi'(\lambda)\xi(\lambda)\,d\lambda\); the latter is said to define \(\xi\).''',r'''If \(V=H-H_0\) is bounded self-adjoint and trace class, there is a unique real \(\xi\in L^1(\mathbb R)\) with \(\operatorname{Tr}(f(H)-f(H_0))=\int f'(\lambda)\xi(\lambda)\,d\lambda\) for smooth compactly supported \(f\), and \(\int\xi=\operatorname{Tr}V\). Almost everywhere on the absolutely continuous spectrum, \(S(\lambda)-I\in\mathfrak S_1\) and its Fredholm determinant equals \(\exp(-2\pi i\xi(\lambda))\). For merely resolvent-comparable pairs one uses a weighted-integrable spectral shift with an additional normalization; the determinant alone determines it only modulo integers.''',r'''A sufficient test-function class and the normalization of the trace-class case are specified. The sign in the scan agrees with the convention \(S=W_+^*W_-\) and with the cited paper.''','E',r'''\citet[\S4, Theorem 3 and formulas (12)--(13), pp.477--478]{birmanKrein1962wave} contains the spectral-shift relation and determinant formula, with the distinction between trace-class and resolvent-comparable normalization.''',r'''established; equality holds almost everywhere and the determinant is the Fredholm determinant, not a finite-matrix determinant unless multiplicity is finite.''',refs=['birmanKrein1962wave'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000412','theorem','Localized trace-class conditions for existence',r'''With \(D(H)=D(H_0)\), the notes require \((H-H_0)E^0(\Delta)\in\mathcal J_1\) for every bounded interval \(\Delta\), and conclude existence of wave operators.''',r'''Suppose self-adjoint \(H,H_0\) act on one Hilbert space with equal domains, and \((H-H_0)E_{H_0}(\Delta)\) extends to a trace-class operator for every bounded interval \(\Delta\). Then \(W_\pm(H,H_0)\) exist as isometries on \(\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)\). This one-sided condition yields existence; completeness follows, for example, if the corresponding reverse conditions also hold, so both inverse wave maps exist.''',r'''The bounded extensions and the distinction between existence and completeness are stated. The spectral projections exhaust the absolutely continuous subspace and provide the increasing family used in the cited theorem.''','E',r'''This is the identity-identification specialization of \citet[Theorem 5.1, p.1126]{belopolskiiBirman1968wave}, received March 1968 and published that year.''',r'''established; the lecture correctly labels this conclusion as existence, not automatically completeness.''',refs=['belopolskiiBirman1968wave'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Scattering theory')
item(252,'P000412','theorem','Two-space wave operators and the identification operator',r'''For different Hilbert spaces the notes write \(U_\pm=\lim e^{itH}Je^{-itH_0}\), retain \(D(H)=D(H_0)\), and require localized \(JH_0-HJ\) to be nuclear and \((J^*J-I)E^0(\Delta)\) compact. The final remark says that choosing \(J=U_+\) is trivial, so one must approximately guess the identification.''',r'''Let \(H_0,H\) be self-adjoint on \(\mathcal H_0,\mathcal H\), and let \(J:\mathcal H_0\to\mathcal H\) be bounded with \(J D(H_0)\subset D(H)\). Suppose for every bounded interval \(\Delta\) that \((HJ-JH_0)E_{H_0}(\Delta)\) has a trace-class extension and \((J^*J-I)E_{H_0}(\Delta)\) is compact. Then \(W_\pm(H,H_0;J)=\operatorname{s-lim}e^{itH}Je^{-itH_0}P_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)\) exist and are isometric on \(\mathcal H_{\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)\). Completeness requires further hypotheses, such as suitable reverse comparison conditions.''',r'''Domain equality across different spaces is replaced by the required domain compatibility. These hypotheses are a convenient stronger specialization of the theorem's exhausting projections. The last sentence is a method for choosing \(J\), not an existence proof using an operator not yet constructed.''','E',r'''\citet[Theorem 5.1, pp.1126--1127]{belopolskiiBirman1968wave} proves the existence and isometry assertion; its Theorem 5.2 supplies stronger conditions for unitarity onto the absolutely continuous part. The manuscript was received on 20 March 1968, four years before the talk.''',r'''established two-Hilbert-space scattering theory, with domain and completeness issues separated.''',refs=['belopolskiiBirman1968wave'],literal='false as written',match='partial',area='Scattering theory')
corr('P000411','description of K_0 as exponentially decaying solutions','K_0 consists of solutions decaying exponentially in time near the boundary.','Distinguish local or compressed evolution from conserved full energy; exponential decay needs additional hypotheses.','Scan inspected. The same page explicitly conserves the global energy, precluding nonzero decay in that norm.')
corr('P000412','Birman functional-calculus trace-ideal line',r'\(\varphi(H)-\varphi(H_0)\) nuclear without restrictions on \(\varphi\).','Specify an admissible invariance-principle function or use the resolvent trace-class criterion.','A constant function satisfies the displayed condition for arbitrary pairs, so omitted hypotheses cannot be dropped.')
corr('P000412','two-space wave-operator condition 1',r'\(D(H)=D(H_0)\)',r'For different spaces require \(J D(H_0)\subseteq D(H)\), with the localized commutator condition.','Scan inspected: the equality is retained after introducing different spaces. Belopolskii--Birman Theorem 5.1 formulates domain compatibility through the identification.')
save()

# Refine the universal-relation formulation: nonunital Steinberg kernels
# must not be silently identified with reduced K_2 without hypotheses.
x=next(x for x in items if x['id']=='1971-247.5')
x['statement']=r'''A precise augmented formulation is as follows. Let \(R=\mathbb Z\langle x_s:s\in S\rangle\) be the free associative unital ring, with augmentation \(\epsilon:R\to\mathbb Z\) sending every variable to zero. Then \(\ker(K_2(R)\xrightarrow{\epsilon_*}K_2(\mathbb Z))=0\). Consequently a stable word in elementary generators that is the identity as a universal matrix and whose Steinberg lift becomes the identity after augmentation is already a consequence of the Steinberg relations. In particular this applies when every entry has zero constant term. Allowing arbitrary constant relations without this normalization introduces the nonzero group \(K_2(\mathbb Z)\).'''
x['reconstruction']=r'''The augmentation condition is reconstructed from the subsequent use of free rings without identity. It expresses reduced \(K_2\)-vanishing without identifying the naive Steinberg kernel of an arbitrary nonunital ring with that reduced group. The cryptic discrete-group subproblem is not identifiable.'''
x['fate']=r'''Vaserstein announces the answer on P000413. \citet[second theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations} states free nonunital acyclicity; \citet[Theorem 28.1 and Corollary 28.1]{vaserstein1976foundations} gives precisely \(K_n(\mathbb Z)\cong K_n(\mathbb Z\langle S\rangle)\) and its reduced consequence, with Gersten's proof cited for higher degrees. The supplied earlier-year index contains no exact recurrence. The match is partial because the February wording does not spell out the augmentation convention.'''
x['present_status']=r'''As of November 2026: the augmented universal-relation statement is resolved. The literal unnormalized statement must be qualified; it is not a remaining open problem.'''
# The publisher's preview actually contains the primary preface and introduction.
x=next(x for x in items if x['id']=='1971-252.1')
