talk(253,'1972-03-06','L. N. Vaserstein','P000413-P000416','Comparison of K-theories, suspension rings, and Laurent boundaries',r'''This substantial lecture compares Volodin's simplicial construction with the derived-functor theories of Gersten and Swan, announces the answer to the elementary-relation question, and develops infinite-matrix suspension, negative groups, relative groups, and polynomial Nil terms. Quillen's comparison is still presented as Vaserstein's expectation here. The final Laurent construction uses Whitehead's elementary factorization and one-sided compression. Many assertions reappear, with fuller definitions and proofs, in Vaserstein's 1973 announcement and 1976 article. The explicit Kazhdan heading at the bottom of P000416 ends this record.''')
item(253,'P000413','construction','Volodin’s simplicial general linear group',r'''The first theory is written \(K_n=\pi_{n-1}(GL(A))\), with a marginal attribution to Volodin and a reference to an abstract simplicial scheme. The first two groups are said to agree with Whitehead and Milnor.''',r'''For a unital ring \(A\), give the stable group \(GL(A)\) the simplicial structure whose finite simplices lie in a left coset of a simultaneously triangular unipotent subgroup, allowing finite permutations of the basis. If \(S(A)\) denotes its realization, Volodin's model has \(K_n^V(A)=\pi_{n-1}S(A)\) for \(n\ge1\), with components for \(n=1\). This is not ordinary homotopy of the discrete group, whose positive homotopy groups would vanish.''',r'''The simplicial topology and degree shift are supplied from the named model; the displayed symbol \(GL(A)\) is shorthand for this extra structure.''','E',r'''The construction was published in \citet[\S\S1--3]{volodin1971extraordinary}, received in July 1970. The explicit scheme convention and comparisons are given in \citet[\S\S11--15]{vaserstein1976foundations}.''',r'''an established model equivalent to Quillen's theory in the unital setting; the later comparison is separately indexed below.''',refs=['volodin1971extraordinary','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(253,'P000413','construction','Gersten–Swan derived functors and free simplicial resolutions',r'''The source names Swan and ``Gershtein'', applies \(GL\) to a free-ring resolution, and observes that exactness is lost. It explicitly chooses rings without identity, \(F(A)=\mathbb Z\langle A\rangle_+\), the noncommutative polynomials without a constant term.''',r'''The free nonunital ring on the underlying set of \(A\) maps onto \(A\) by evaluation. Iterating the free-ring adjunction produces a simplicial free resolution. Applying stable linear or elementary-group functors gives the nonabelian derived-functor constructions associated with Gersten and Swan; a three-term sequence alone does not define all their derived groups. The unitization of \(F(A)\) is the unital free ring \(\mathbb Z\langle A\rangle\).''',r'''The surname is normalized to Gersten. The full simplicial resolution is distinguished from the abbreviated chain in the notes, and the omitted identity is a substantive convention rather than a harmless typographical omission.''','E',r'''\citet[\S29, pp.142--143]{vaserstein1976foundations} identifies the antecedent constructions as Swan (1970) and Gersten (1971), and explains their equivalence via Keune's formulations. This verifies the framework retrospectively; the notebook is not the first definition.''',r'''established antecedent constructions, with the later comparison stated using a suitable fibre-product functor in the next items.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(253,'P000413','theorem','Acyclicity of free rings in reduced K-theory',r'''The notes say \(K_1\) of a free ring is zero, and propose induction from an exact free presentation to define the higher groups.''',r'''For any set \(S\), the inclusion \(\mathbb Z\to R=\mathbb Z\langle S\rangle\) induces \(K_n(\mathbb Z)\cong K_n(R)\) for all integers \(n\), using nonconnective \(K\)-theory in negative degrees. Thus the augmented or reduced theory of \(R\), equivalently the chosen theory of its free nonunital augmentation ideal, vanishes. The unreduced statement \(K_1(R)=0\) is false: it contains \(K_1(\mathbb Z)\cong\{\pm1\}\).''',r'''The reduced convention already indicated by ``without identity'' is made explicit. The full all-degree theorem is the published form of the lecture's acyclicity mechanism, not an assertion that the three written arrows prove it.''','A',r'''\citet[second theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations} announces the vanishing; \citet[Theorem 28.1 and Corollary 28.1, p.142]{vaserstein1976foundations} states the precise unital and reduced forms. The latter cites Gersten's higher-degree theorem and explicitly does not reproduce its long proof.''',r'''established. The 1973 note was received on 14 November 1972; the 1976 article supplies the exact formulation and references for its proof.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(253,'P000413','claim','The announced solution of the universal Steinberg-relation problem',r'''Vaserstein says that he has solved the problem about Steinberg relations and that the solution follows from comparison with Volodin's functors.''',r'''For the augmented free ring \(R=\mathbb Z\langle S\rangle\), the kernel of \(K_2(R)\to K_2(\mathbb Z)\) vanishes. Hence a universally valid matrix relation whose Steinberg word specializes to the identity when all variables are zero is generated by Steinberg relations. This is the normalized precise version of the question recorded on P000401.''',r'''The statement uses the augmentation convention established in item 1971-247.5. Neither vanishing of \(K_2\) for every ring nor a presentation of every finite-rank elementary group follows.''','A',r'''This occurrence is the notebook's answer to 1971-247.5. The free-ring theorem in \citet[second theorem]{vaserstein1973foundations} and \citet[Theorem 28.1]{vaserstein1976foundations} supports that answer, with the reconstructed normalization stated explicitly.''',r'''resolved for the normalized universal question; it is retained in the problem index to connect the February question with the March answer.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',problem='GP-1971-20')
item(253,'P000413-P000414','construction','Cone matrices, Morita invariance, and suspension',r'''The ring \(MA\) has finitely many nonzero entries in total; \(\ell A\) has finitely many in each row and each column. The notes assert \(K_n(\ell A)=0\), define \(\mu A=\ell A/MA\), and obtain \(K_n(A)=K_n(MA)=K_{n+1}(\mu A)\). The proof is compared with Kuiper's theorem.''',r'''Let \(C(A)\) be the ring of countable row-and-column-finite matrices over a unital associative ring \(A\), and let \(M_\infty(A)\) be its ideal of finitely supported matrices. Put \(\Sigma A=C(A)/M_\infty(A)\). In the algebraic suspension construction, \(K_n(C(A))=0\), \(K_n(M_\infty(A))\cong K_n(A)\), and \(K_{n+1}(\Sigma A)\cong K_n(A)\), in all integer degrees after negative groups have been included.''',r'''These are precisely the matrix classes in the 1973 announcement. No finite-band restriction is imposed on this cone ring: the bandwidth condition in record 247 applies to multiplicativity of a different two-sided truncation. Contractibility here means vanishing in the specified \(K\)-theory, not contractibility of a discrete matrix group as a topological space. The locally unital finite-matrix ideal permits the suspension exact sequence.''','A',r'''\citet[first theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations} gives these identities with the stated cone definition; \citet[\S\S18--21]{vaserstein1976foundations} supplies the asphericity, Morita, and suspension arguments. These are part of a contemporary algebraic suspension programme, not a claim that infinite matrices first entered \(K\)-theory in this lecture.''',r'''established. The analytic analogy with Kuiper does not change the algebraic definition or provide the missing proof by itself.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(253,'P000414','construction','Recovering K0 and Bass’s negative groups',r'''The suspension formula yields the ordinary full \(K_0\), with a free module nonzero, by \(K_0(A)=K_1(\mu A)\). Iteration extends \(K_n\) to all integers and is said to coincide with Bass's groups.''',r'''The suspension model gives \(K_0(A)\cong K_1(\Sigma A)\) and \(K_{-r}(A)\cong K_0(\Sigma^r A)\) for \(r\ge1\). Here \(K_0\) is the full Grothendieck group of finitely generated projectives, not the quotient by free modules. The resulting negative groups agree with Bass's negative \(K\)-groups; for regular noetherian rings they vanish.''',r'''The phrase about a free module excludes reduced \(\widetilde K_0\). The regular-noetherian vanishing is supplied as established context, not claimed to be written on P000414.''','E',r'''Bass's negative theory predates the talk (1968); its comparison with the suspension definition is explicitly recorded in \citet[first theorem and references 10--12]{vaserstein1973foundations} and proved in the framework of \citet[\S21]{vaserstein1976foundations}.''',r'''standard nonconnective extension. Positive-degree polynomial invariance does not follow merely from the existence of these negative groups.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(253,'P000414-P000415','construction','Polynomial Nil terms and the distinction from Karoubi–Villamayor theory',r'''The notes define \(\operatorname{Nil}_n A=K_n(tA[t])\), contrast this with a theory that contracts \(tA[t]\), and use \(t(t-1)A[t]\to tA[t]\xrightarrow{t=1}A\) as Karoubi's loop construction.''',r'''For a unital ring, define \(NK_n(A)=\ker(K_n(A[t])\xrightarrow{t=0}K_n(A))\). The constant inclusion splits evaluation, so \(K_n(A[t])\cong K_n(A)\oplus NK_n(A)\). Interpreting the notebook's \(K_n(tA[t])\) requires the corresponding augmented or relative convention. Polynomial homotopy theories force the polynomial path ring to be contractible; ordinary algebraic \(K\)-theory can retain its Nil contribution. The kernel of evaluation at one on \(tA[t]\) is \(t(t-1)A[t]\).''',r'''The modern \(NK\) notation distinguishes these groups from differently indexed categories of nilpotent endomorphisms. The equality with a nonunital absolute functor is not used as an assertion of excision for arbitrary ideals.''','A',r'''\citet[third theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations} states the polynomial and Laurent decompositions with these Nil terms. The distinction from polynomial-path \(K\)-theory is discussed in \citet[Introduction, p.96]{vaserstein1976foundations}; the underlying Karoubi--Villamayor theory had circulated before the lecture.''',r'''established distinction; Nil groups are exactly the obstruction in this polynomial splitting, not an omission to be discarded for every ring.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(253,'P000413-P000414','construction','Relative groups and the correction from kernels to fibre products',r'''The source first writes \(K_{n+1}(A)=K_n(A'')\) after calling \(A''\) the kernel of a free presentation. It later proposes a pullback ring \(F\) giving an exact sequence \(\cdots\to K_n(A)\to K_n(B)\to K_n(F)\to K_{n-1}(A)\to\cdots\), with an ambiguous \(\mu B\)-label on an inclusion into \(\ell B\).''',r'''For a homomorphism \(f:A\to B\), define relative \(K\)-theory by the homotopy fibre of \(K(A)\to K(B)\); its groups fit \(\cdots\to K_n(A,B)\to K_n(A)\to K_n(B)\to K_{n-1}(A,B)\to\cdots\). In Vaserstein's ring model let \(F(B)\to B\) be the free nonunital presentation and put \(D_f=A\times_B F(B)\). His relative group is represented by \(K_n(D_f)\). For dimension shifting he uses \(D(A)=F(A)\times_A F(A)\), with \(K_n(D(A))\cong K_{n+1}(A)\).''',r'''The fibre sequence fixes the indexing: a ring sitting after \(K_n(B)\) in the notes represents a shifted relative object. The published doubled fibre product is not simply the presentation kernel. The drawn inclusion cannot have the earlier quotient \(\mu B\) as its evident source; its uncertain letter is left unresolved, and the verified published model is supplied separately.''','A',r'''\citet[second theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations} gives the relative pullback model. \citet[\S20, Theorem 29.1 and its proof, pp.142--143]{vaserstein1976foundations} identifies the doubled fibre product and proves dimension shifting. General excision is not assumed.''',r'''established relative and dimension-shift constructions. The literal kernel recursion and the unqualified pullback diagram are not sufficient definitions.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(253,'P000415','claim','The plus construction does not automatically make every group an H-space',r'''For any group \(G\) with perfect commutator subgroup \(E\), the notes attach cells to \(BG\), kill \(E\), preserve homology, and say that this turns the result into a group or H-space.''',r'''The plus construction \(BG\to BG_E^+\) kills the specified perfect normal subgroup and preserves homology with the appropriate local coefficients. This operation alone does not provide an H-space multiplication for every such \(G\). For the stable general linear group, compatible block sums supply the additional multiplication, and Quillen's positive groups are \(\pi_n(BGL(A)^+)\).''',r'''The general existence of plus is separated from the stable-linear source of its multiplication. Homotopy groups of a general plus space are not automatically ring \(K\)-groups without a ring and the relevant model.''','E',r'''Quillen's already circulated account \citep[\S3, p.50]{quillen1971cohomology} treats the stable general linear group and its coherent categorical structure. The general plus theorem is given with its correct hypotheses in \citet[IV, Theorem 1.5]{weibel2013kbook}.''',r'''standard after this distinction; the universal H-space claim is an overstatement in the notebook.''',refs=['quillen1971cohomology','weibel2013kbook'],literal='false as written',match='partial')
item(253,'P000415','remark','The assertion that everything extends to the unitary case',r'''One emphatic sentence says that everything carries over to the unitary case.''',r'''The preceding discussion contains several inequivalent assertions: plus constructions, comparison of models, suspension, relative groups, and Laurent decompositions. A unitary or hermitian version must specify a ring with involution, symmetry and form parameter, degree conventions, and often additional hypotheses. None is supplied by this sentence.''',r'''It is therefore not interpreted as a single theorem asserting all earlier formulas unchanged for every hermitian theory. It continues the programme of record 246, rather than settling every possible hermitian analogue.''','F',r'''The verified \citet{vaserstein1973foundations,vaserstein1976foundations} concern ordinary algebraic \(K\)-theory. Their linear comparison theorem does not verify this unrestricted unitary extension. The earlier form-ring work \citep{vaserstein1970stabilization} supports the setting but not the claimed all-degree package.''',r'''the exact package intended remains underdetermined; no claim that hermitian \(K\)-theory itself was left undeveloped is made.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations','vaserstein1970stabilization'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',area='Hermitian K-theory')
item(253,'P000415','theorem','The higher Laurent fundamental theorem and its missing summands',r'''A theorem attributed to an uncertain surname resembling Wagoner is written \(K_2(A[t,t^{-1}])=K_2(A)\oplus K_1(A)\oplus ?\). It is asserted for all \(n\), with the other degrees following from this case.''',r'''For a unital associative ring and central Laurent variable, the nonconnective fundamental theorem is \[K_n(A[t,t^{-1}])\cong K_n(A)\oplus K_{n-1}(A)\oplus NK_n(A)\oplus NK_n(A),\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.\] For regular noetherian \(A\), the two Nil groups vanish. The constant inclusion is split by evaluation at \(t=1\); it does not make the remaining relative group zero.''',r'''The circled final mark remains a question mark diplomatically; the two Nil terms are supplied from the precise theorem. The arrow from \(A\) means inclusion as constant polynomials, not the coefficient-extraction map from Laurent polynomials, which is not a ring homomorphism.''','A',r'''The full formula occurs in \citet[third theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations}. Its prior low-degree antecedents are \citet{basshellerswan1964whitehead,gersten1971laurent}. Wagoner is a plausible reading of the name, supported by the contemporary suspension literature, but the scan does not identify a precise cited paper; no priority claim rests on that reading.''',r'''established, with two Nil terms for general rings. The claim that a written relative \(K_2\) is simply trivial requires correction.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','basshellerswan1964whitehead','gersten1971laurent'],literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(253,'P000415','construction','Whitehead’s four factors and the Laurent K1-to-K2 class',r'''The diagonal block \(\operatorname{diag}(a,a^{-1})\) is written as four elementary matrices, with two entries left as dots. The notes then place the diagonal blocks for \(gt\), \(t\), and \(g\) side by side, and say that their elementary decompositions give a relation, hence an element of \(K_2\).''',r'''For an invertible matrix \(a\), put \(U(b)=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&b\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\) and \(L(c)=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&0\\c&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). Then \(\operatorname{diag}(a,a^{-1})=U(a-1)L(1)U(a^{-1}-1)L(-a)\). If \(h(a)\) is the corresponding Steinberg lift and \(t\) is central, the product \(h(gt)h(t)^{-1}h(g)^{-1}\) maps to the identity and so defines a \(K_2(A[t,t^{-1}])\)-class. With compatible conventions this is the Laurent splitting from \(K_1(A)\).''',r'''The two entries are uniquely recovered by multiplication. The inverses and multiplication order between the three lifts are supplied; without them the three displayed matrices do not themselves constitute a relation. Well-definedness on \(K_1\) is part of the established splitting theorem, not proved by the shorthand alone.''','E',r'''The elementary identity is Whitehead's classical stabilization device. The low-degree Laurent map is part of the theory discussed in \citet{milnor1972introduction,gersten1971laurent}; the full splitting is explicitly recorded by \citet[third theorem]{vaserstein1973foundations}. Milnor's book was published in January 1972 according to the publisher catalogue.''',r'''established. The restored factors are an elementary verification of the written construction, not a new research contribution.''',refs=['milnor1972introduction','gersten1971laurent','vaserstein1973foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(253,'P000416','method','Compression of Laurent relations and the inverse boundary',r'''For the converse, Laurent multiplication is made into an infinite matrix, cut to one side, and considered modulo finite matrices. The quotient is written \(\ell A/\mu A\), although \(\mu A\) was previously the quotient ring.''',r'''Laurent polynomial matrices act as two-sided finite-band matrices. Compression to nonnegative indices is a ring homomorphism modulo the ideal of finitely supported matrices, because the multiplication defect has finite support. A \(K_2\)-relation can be lifted through the resulting extension; its boundary is a \(K_1(A)\)-class after Morita identification of the finite-matrix ideal. The quotient intended with the earlier notation is \(\ell A/MA=\mu A\).''',r'''The method is the preceding record's truncation construction, now used in the reverse Laurent map. The word Laurent series cannot automatically mean arbitrary formal series: their matrices need not belong to the row-and-column-finite algebra being used. The safe algebraic domain here is Laurent polynomials or an explicitly enlarged topological matrix algebra, not supplied by the notes.''','A',r'''The precise suspension and exact-sequence context is \citet[first--third theorems]{vaserstein1973foundations} and \citet[\S\S18--20]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The notebook supplies an implementation sketch, so the publication match is partial.''',r'''established as the algebraic boundary after the matrix class and ideal are fixed; the notation drift is not a new quotient construction.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='medium')
corr('P000413','derived-functor author name','Swan; Gershtein','Swan; Gersten (S. M. Gersten).','The named construction matches Gersten’s 1971 higher K-functor papers; the phonetic notebook form is normalized in commentary.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000413','free-presentation recursion',r'\(K_{n+1}(A)=K_n(A\prime\prime)\), where \(A\prime\prime\) was called the presentation kernel.',r'The published dimension shift uses \(D(A)=F(A)\times_A F(A)\), or relative groups, not an unqualified absolute theory of the kernel.','Vaserstein 1976, §29, Theorem 29.1 gives the precise doubled fibre-product construction. General excision for arbitrary ideals must not be assumed.')
corr('P000414','relative pullback diagram','The source of the inclusion into the cone is labelled mu B (possibly mB or MB).','Leave the letter uncertain; it cannot be the previously defined quotient mu B with its evident inclusion.','Scan inspected. The published relative-ring model is A times_B F(B); the notebook diagram and its shifted indexing are not identified silently.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000415','general plus-construction description','For any group with perfect commutator subgroup, cell attachments turn BG into an H-space.','The plus construction exists; an H-space multiplication needs further structure, available for stable GL through block sum.','Scan inspected. The line explicitly says any group and then H-space. Quillen ICM §3 supplies the stable-linear categorical structure.')
corr('P000415','Laurent splitting and relative-group caption','K_2 relative is trivial.','The evaluation map is split, but its relative kernel contains the K_1 and Nil contributions.','Scan inspected. The same displayed Laurent decomposition already contains a K_1 summand.')
corr('P000415','Whitehead matrix factorization, two blank entries','Third upper-right and fourth lower-left entries are dots.','They are a^{-1}-1 and -a, respectively.','Direct multiplication restores diag(a,a^{-1}) for any invertible a; this is a mathematical completion, not a claim that the notebook wrote these entries.',typ='ambiguity')
