talk(256,'1972-03-13','L. N. Vaserstein','P000420-P000422','Simplicial K-theory, algebraic suspension, and comparisons',r'''The explicit Vaserstein heading in the lower part of P000420 begins a description of triangular simplices in the stable general linear group. The next page constructs an algebraic cone and suspension, states their degree-shifting property, returns to free-ring resolutions, and compares negative groups with Bass's. The first two lines of P000422 concern Karoubi's polynomial theory. The subsequent unheaded Quillen comparison block is allocated conditionally to catalogue record 257; there is no additional dated Kazhdan heading at that boundary. Two significant qualifications are needed: relative K-theory cannot generally be replaced by the K-theory of a bare kernel, and absence of zero divisors does not imply agreement with polynomial-homotopy K-theory.''')
item(256,'P000420-P000421','construction','Triangular simplices on the stable general linear group',r'''An abstract simplicial scheme is a collection of finite subsets closed under passage to subsets. On stable matrices the notes declare a finite set \(a_1,\ldots,a_n\) to be a simplex if simultaneous left and right multiplication by invertible matrices makes them upper unitriangular. Conjugates, permutation matrices, and a marginal reference to Volodin accompany \(K_i(A)=\pi_{i-1}GL(A)\).''',r'''For an associative unital ring \(A\), one equips the set \(GL(A)=\varinjlim_nGL_n(A)\) with the triangular simplicial structure, rather than its discrete topology. A finite subset contained in a translate of an admissible conjugate of a unitriangular subgroup determines a simplex. In the usual Volodin version the triangular subgroups are indexed by orderings of a finite basis. The realization \(S(A)\) of the specified stable complex satisfies \(K_i(A)\cong\pi_{i-1}S(A)\) for \(i\ge1\), with \(\pi_0\) carrying its stable group structure.''',r'''The source's two-sided formulation and its marginal restrictions are recorded without claiming that each possible choice of conjugating matrices gives literally the same complex. The standard Volodin model is the precise comparator. The homotopy groups belong to a realization, not to an ordinary discrete general linear group.''','E',r'''The construction had appeared in \citet{volodin1971extraordinary}, received on 27 July 1970 and published in 1971. Its relationship with Vaserstein's relative formulation is specified in \citet[\S15, Theorem 15.1 and Corollary 15.1]{vaserstein1976foundations}; the later comparison with Quillen is a separate theorem, indexed under record 257.''',r'''the simplicial models are established. The unreadable adjective in the margin is not used to impose a new matrix restriction.''',refs=['volodin1971extraordinary','vaserstein1976foundations'],match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(256,'P000420-P000421','construction','The algebraic cone and suspension rings',r'''The notation \(\ell A\) denotes infinite matrices with finite rows and columns; \(MA\) denotes the finite matrices, and \(\mu A=\ell A/MA\) is called the ring of Fredholm operators.''',r'''Let \(C(A)\) be the ring of countably indexed matrices over \(A\) having finitely many nonzero entries in each row and each column, and let \(M_\infty(A)\) be its ideal of matrices with finite total support. Set \(\Sigma A=C(A)/M_\infty(A)\). Matrix multiplication is well-defined under these finiteness conditions. The invertible classes in the quotient are algebraic Fredholm classes; arbitrary elements of the quotient need not be invertible.''',r'''The symbols \(C\) and \(\Sigma\) replace the source's \(\ell\) and \(\mu\). This purely algebraic quotient is analogous to a Calkin algebra. Even over \(\mathbb C\), row and column finiteness alone does not ensure that the matrix defines a bounded operator on \(\ell^2\).''','E',r'''The matrix cone is treated in \citet[\S\S3--4 and Chapter IV]{vaserstein1976foundations}. Its historical introduction credits earlier Fredholm-operator definitions of negative K-groups to Karoubi--Villamayor and Wagoner; the 1972 talk reuses that algebraic construction.''',r'''standard algebraic suspension construction, with the ring and its group of invertible classes distinguished.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations'],match='exact')
item(256,'P000421','theorem','Suspension, vanishing of cone K-theory, and the index',r'''The framed theorem is \(K_iA=K_{i+1}\mu A\), in particular \(K_1\mu A=K_0A\), called the index. Its explanation is contractibility of \(GL(\ell A)\), or at least vanishing of \(K_i(\ell A)\). A separate sentence asks whether this holds for Quillen's functor.''',r'''For a unital associative ring \(A\), the algebraic cone is flasque: the stable sum swindle gives \(K_n(C(A))=0\). The extension \(M_\infty(A)\to C(A)\to\Sigma A\), together with Morita invariance and its applicable localization sequence, gives \[K_{n+1}(\Sigma A)\xrightarrow{\ \partial\ }K_n(A)\] as an isomorphism for \(n\ge0\), and for every integer \(n\) in nonconnective K-theory. For \(n=0\), \(\partial\) is the algebraic index.''',r'''Contractibility refers to the chosen K-theory or simplicial model; it is not a claim that the underlying discrete ring or group is contractible. The special matrix ideal supplies the exactness required here, without asserting excision for arbitrary ideals. The exercise about finding the arrows is interpreted as supplying these boundary and comparison maps, not as an additional specified problem.''','A',r'''The degree shift and comparison are announced in \citet[the second and third theorem blocks]{vaserstein1973foundations}, received on 14 November 1972, and developed in \citet[Chapter IV, especially \S18; \S27]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The latter explicitly dates the comparison programme to Gelfand's seminar in spring 1972. The same degree shift holds in Quillen K-theory under the displayed hypotheses.''',r'''resolved. The question about Quillen's theory is an instance of the model-comparison problem GP-1971-21.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],problem='GP-1971-21')
item(256,'P000421','remark','The unspecified Banach-ring generalization',r'''One line says that the theorem generalizes to a Banach ring and gives the usual theorem. No topology, completed ideal, or K-theory convention is named.''',r'''A Banach-ring analogue must specify the category of modules or operators, its completion, the ideal being divided out, and whether K-theory uses norm-continuous paths or algebraic plus constructions. The preceding row-and-column-finite ring is not, as defined, a Banach algebra. Thus this line does not determine a single comparison or periodicity theorem.''',r'''An analytic Fredholm-index theorem is a plausible context, but identifying it with a particular completed Calkin extension would supply data not present in the source. Algebraic and topological K-groups of a Banach algebra are not silently equated.''','F',r'''The verified matrix construction in \citet[Chapter IV]{vaserstein1976foundations} and the searches for Karoubi--Villamayor Banach-ring versions provide context, but no exact Banach theorem with the notebook's omitted choices was identified.''',r'''unidentified at the required level of hypotheses; the ambiguity does not establish an open analytic problem.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
item(256,'P000421','theorem','Free rings are K-acyclic only in the reduced convention',r'''A framed statement says \(K_i(\text{free ring})=0\), followed immediately by the warning that there is no contraction of the free ring.''',r'''If \(F\) is a free associative nonunital ring on a set \(X\), its unitization is \(\mathbb Z\langle X\rangle\). The relevant assertion is \[\ker\!\left(K_n(\mathbb Z\langle X\rangle)\longrightarrow K_n(\mathbb Z)\right)=0.\] Equivalently the inclusion of constants induces \(K_n(\mathbb Z)\cong K_n(\mathbb Z\langle X\rangle)\). This holds for all integers in the nonconnective extension. It does not assert \(K_0(\mathbb Z\langle X\rangle)=0\), nor a contraction by ring homomorphisms.''',r'''The augmentation and reduced interpretation are supplied from the surrounding free nonunital resolution. No excision identification with an arbitrary ambient relative group is required. This is the same acyclicity used in occurrences 1971-253.3--1971-253.4.''','A',r'''See \citet[the fourth theorem block]{vaserstein1973foundations}, followed by \citet[Theorem 28.1 and Corollary 28.1]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The detailed article explains that the higher-degree argument uses Gersten's results and does not reproduce that long proof. The 1972 announcement and the 1973/1976 publications are therefore distinguished.''',r'''established in the stated reduced sense. The free-ring vanishing supports the normalized universal-relation question GP-1971-20, but is stronger than that degree-two instance.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],problem='GP-1971-20')
item(256,'P000421','method','Free resolutions and the required relative degree shift',r'''For \(0\to L'\to L\to A\to0\), with \(L\) free, the notes write \(K_iA=K_{i-1}L'\), refer to a long exact sequence, and say that induction gives the theories of Swan and Gersten.''',r'''Let \(F(A)\twoheadrightarrow A\) be a functorial free nonunital presentation. The homotopy fibre of \(K(F(A))\to K(A)\) has homotopy groups \(K_{n+1}(A)\), since the reduced K-space of \(F(A)\) is contractible. It must be modeled as relative K-theory. In Vaserstein's explicit ring recursion, the doubled fibre product \(D(A)=F(A)\times_A F(A)\), together with the split projection to \(F(A)\), supplies the functor used to shift degrees. The assertion for the bare kernel \(L'\) does not follow from free acyclicity alone.''',r'''The relative fibre replaces the source's unrestricted use of \(K(L')\). This is the same excision issue as occurrence 1971-253.8. A long exact sequence is legitimate for the homotopy fibre by definition; identifying its terms with absolute K-groups of an ideal needs an additional theorem.''','A',r'''\citet[\S29, definition preceding Theorem 29.1 and Theorem 29.1]{vaserstein1976foundations} gives the precise doubled-ring recursion and comparison with Gersten and Swan. The comparison was announced in \citet{vaserstein1973foundations}, following the spring 1972 seminar.''',r'''the corrected relative construction is established. The bare-kernel shortcut remains an unjustified literal statement in the notes.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations','vaserstein1973foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(256,'P000421','theorem','Agreement with Bass negative groups and regular vanishing',r'''The final lines say that the groups with negative index also coincide with Bass's, and that all of them vanish for regular rings.''',r'''For a unital ring, the negative groups obtained from repeated algebraic suspension agree with Bass negative K-theory. If \(A\) is regular noetherian, then \(K_n(A)=0\) for all \(n<0\). Here regular means the homological regularity relevant to the fundamental theorem, not merely absence of zero divisors.''',r'''The regular noetherian hypothesis is made explicit to avoid extending the assertion to arbitrary singular domains. The equality concerns negative K-groups, separately from the positive polynomial-homotopy comparison on P000422.''','E',r'''Bass's negative construction predates the lecture; its relationship with the Fredholm model is recalled in \citet[introduction, pp.95--96 and Chapter IV]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The modern nonconnective formulation is \citet[IV, \S10]{weibel2013kbook}; the regularity convention is already explicit in \citet{basshellerswan1964whitehead}.''',r'''classical. Singular rings can have nonzero negative K-groups, so the regularity qualification carries mathematical content.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations','weibel2013kbook','basshellerswan1964whitehead'])
item(256,'P000422','claim','Karoubi–Villamayor comparison: a domain hypothesis is insufficient',r'''The first two lines say that Karoubi's positive-index theory differs from Bass's, but coincides when there are no zero divisors: \ru{Нет делит. нуля}.''',r'''For a unital ring \(A\), polynomial-homotopy K-theory is \(KV_n(A)=\pi_{n-1}GL(A[\Delta^\bullet])\), \(n\ge1\). The natural comparison \(K_n(A)\to KV_n(A)\) is an isomorphism for regular noetherian rings. It is not an isomorphism for all integral domains. For example, the graded domain \(A=\mathbb F_p[t^2,t^3]\) has \(KV_2(A)=KV_2(\mathbb F_p)=0\), whereas \(K_2(A,(t^2,t^3))\ne0\) and hence \(K_2(A)\ne0\).''',r'''The intended correct hypothesis is reconstructed as regularity, not read into the words ``no zero divisors''. The cusp is a later explicit diagnostic: polynomial contraction of the positive grading gives the KV equality, while the cited cusp calculation supplies the nonzero relative group. The split augmentation identifies that group with \(K_2(A)\).''','B',r'''The distinction between polynomial paths and stable elementary equivalence predates the talk and is discussed in \citet[introduction, p.96]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The precise regular-ring comparison is \citet[IV, Theorem 11.8]{weibel2013kbook}. A direct modern counterexample to the literal domain claim follows from \citet[Theorem A]{hesselholtnikolaus2020cuspidal}: its Witt-vector quotient for \(a=2,b=3,r=1\) is nonzero. That paper first circulated on 20 March 2019 and was published in 2020; it is not attributed to the 1972 seminar.''',r'''the regular-ring comparison is established, and the unrestricted domain claim is false. This is a correction of hypotheses, not a newly posed unresolved problem.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations','weibel2013kbook','hesselholtnikolaus2020cuspidal'],literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='high')
corr('P000421','K_i(GL) and cone contractibility',r'\(K_iA=\pi_{i-1}GL(A)\); \(GL(\ell A)\) is contractible.',r'Homotopy is taken in the specified simplicial realization, not in the discrete group.','The page explicitly introduces a simplicial scheme, and Vaserstein 1976 §§7–15 makes that structure essential.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000421','free-kernel degree shift',r'\(K_i(A)=K_{i-1}(L\prime)\) for a free presentation.',r'Use the relative homotopy fibre; the published recursion uses the doubled fibre product F(A) ×_A F(A).','Scan inspected; Vaserstein 1976 §29, Theorem 29.1. Free acyclicity does not supply unrestricted excision.',typ='faithful_error')
