# Explicitly label the editorial deduction from the published cusp formula.
_cusp=next(x for x in items if x['id']=='1971-256.8')
_cusp['reconstruction']=r'''The intended correct hypothesis is reconstructed as regularity, not read into the words ``no zero divisors''. \ours\ The diagnostic deduction is as follows. Fix a prime \(p\) and the perfect field \(k=\mathbb F_p\). The ring \(A=k[t^2,t^3]\subset k[t]\) is a domain, and \(t^2\mapsto s^2t^2,\ t^3\mapsto s^3t^3\) defines a polynomial homotopy from the augmentation followed by constants to the identity. Hence \(KV_2(A)=KV_2(k)=K_2(k)=0\). In Hesselholt--Nikolaus's Theorem A, take \(a=2,b=3,r=1\). The truncation set contains 1, and the first Witt coordinate maps its quotient onto \(k\), since the images of both Verschiebung maps \(V_2,V_3\) have zero first coordinate. Thus \(K_2(A,(t^2,t^3))\ne0\). The augmentation is split, so this relative group is a direct summand of \(K_2(A)\); indeed it is the whole group because \(K_2(k)=0\).'''
save()

talk(257,'1972-03-20','D. A. Kazhdan (catalogue attribution; notebook heading absent)','P000422-P000423','Quillen comparison block: conditional attribution',r'''This entry preserves the catalogue's record and date, but its notebook attribution is conditional. The unheaded block beginning ``Quillen's theory'' on P000422 follows Vaserstein's Karoubi comparison, and continues through the upper half of P000423. It explains the plus construction, states equality of the Quillen, Swan, Gersten, Volodin and Vaserstein theories, and sketches a quotient model. Morava is named only for an intervention. The next explicit date and speaker are 20 March and Gelfand below the divider on P000423; Kazhdan's explicit proof heading occurs only on P000424, in record 259. The theorem here belongs to the same comparison programme announced by Vaserstein, so the allocation is not evidence for an independent Kazhdan priority claim.''')
item(257,'P000422','construction','The stable plus construction and its universal property',r'''With \(G=GL(A)\), the notes identify \(E(A)\) with the perfect commutator subgroup, give \(BG\to BG^+\) with fundamental group \(G/E\) and unchanged homology, and state universality for maps to H-spaces. Morava adds that an H-space hypothesis can be dropped when the other map induces a homology isomorphism.''',r'''For a unital associative ring \(A\), \(E(A)=[GL(A),GL(A)]\) is perfect. The plus map \(BGL(A)\to BGL(A)^+\) kills \(E(A)\) and induces isomorphisms in homology with every local coefficient system pulled back from \(K_1(A)\). Its target is a homotopy-commutative H-space, and \(K_i(A)=\pi_iBGL(A)^+\), \(i\ge1\). The corresponding factorization property holds for connected H-space targets. A uniqueness comparison without an H-space assumption must retain the appropriate fundamental-group and acyclic-map hypotheses; an arbitrary integral homology equivalence alone is insufficient.''',r'''Local coefficients and the killed subgroup are supplied to make the plus characterization precise. Morava's abbreviated correction is interpreted only within that setting, not as a blanket homology-Whitehead theorem for all spaces. The crossed equality sign does not alter the verified stable perfectness statement.''','E',r'''Quillen's construction was already reported in \citet[\S3, p.50]{quillen1971cohomology}, from the 1970 ICM lecture, printed in 1971. The precise characterization and universal properties are \citet[IV, Definition 1.1 and Theorems 1.5 and 1.8]{weibel2013kbook}. The explicit discussion continued in occurrence 1971-259.1.''',r'''established. This exposition does not create a new comparison theorem merely because its catalogue attribution is uncertain.''',refs=['quillen1971cohomology','weibel2013kbook'],match='partial')
item(257,'P000422','theorem','Equality of the Quillen, Volodin, Swan, Gersten and Vaserstein groups',r'''The framed theorem states \(K_iA=K_i^QA\), followed by a list \(S,G,\mathrm{Vol}_1,\mathrm{Vol}_2,\mathrm{Vas}\) over the right-hand K-symbol.''',r'''For an associative ring \(A\) with identity and \(i\ge1\), the stable simplicial K-groups of the indicated Volodin and Vaserstein constructions, the free-resolution constructions of Swan and Gersten, and \(\pi_iBGL(A)^+\) are naturally isomorphic, when the nonunital intermediary rings and relative constructions use the respective published definitions. This does not identify Karoubi--Villamayor's polynomial-homotopy theory with Quillen's for arbitrary rings.''',r'''The unital hypothesis and the restriction to positive groups are stated explicitly. The list is treated as a comparison of constructions, not a claim that their underlying spaces are identical before completion or delooping. The preceding Karoubi warning is essential to the scope.''','B',r'''The precise match is \citet[the first theorem block]{vaserstein1973foundations}; the detailed proof is \citet[\S15, \S27, Theorem 27.2, and \S29, Theorem 29.1]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The latter records first announcement in Gelfand's seminar in spring 1972 and in Tbilisi in autumn 1972; the short paper was received on 14 November 1972. Class B is relative to the conditional catalogue speaker Kazhdan; it does not imply an independent announcement distinct from Vaserstein's occurrences 1971-253.14 and 1971-256.3.''',r'''resolved for the specified models and rings. GP-1971-21 links the earlier question and this report without duplicating its mathematical priority.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],problem='GP-1971-21',confidence='medium')
item(257,'P000422-P000423','method','Acyclic coinvariants and the quotient comparison',r'''A diagram compares realization before and after quotienting the cone's simplicial matrix space by \(GL(MA)\). The final quotient is labeled \(BG^+\), with two exclamation marks. Bars and initial B-symbols are overwritten, so the individual nodes are not all diplomatically secure.''',r'''The proof uses an equivariant simplicial model and its chain complex, not an unrestricted rule that taking a quotient commutes with geometric realization. In Vaserstein's formulation, if every finite subset of a ring \(R\) lies in a unital subring, the coinvariant chain complex \((C_*S(R))_{GL(R)}\), with his stable-matrix notation understood, has vanishing positive-degree homology. Applying this acyclicity to the cone comparison, together with the kernel on \(\pi_1\), identifies the resulting connected target as a plus construction.''',r'''The explicitly published acyclicity theorem is supplied as the rigorous content behind the sketch. The point-set quotient, simplicial quotient, and their realizations remain distinct until the hypotheses and comparison maps are verified. No overwritten B-symbol is silently resolved into a different proof.''','B',r'''See \citet[Theorem 26.1, Lemma 27.1 and proof of Theorem 27.2, pp.139--142]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The detailed chain and subdivision argument justifies the spring 1972 programme later announced in \citet{vaserstein1973foundations}; it is more than a formal diagram chase.''',r'''established in the stated locally unital setting, which includes unital rings and the relevant finite matrix ideals. The notebook gives a proof outline, not the required equivariant argument in full.''',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations','vaserstein1973foundations'],match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(257,'P000423','theorem','The identity component and the missing K-zero factor',r'''The page begins \(GL(\mu A)=K_0A\oplus\cdots\), then identifies a simplicial quotient with the identity component of the realized suspension space. The final boxes state \(K_iA=\pi_iGL(\mu A)\) and an equality of its homology with stable group homology.''',r'''For the suspension model \(S(\Sigma A)\), \(\pi_0S(\Sigma A)\cong K_0(A)\), and its identity component is a model of \(BGL(A)^+\). Thus, after choices of component identifications, the full K-space has homotopy type \(K_0(A)\times BGL(A)^+\), while \(K_i(A)=\pi_iS(\Sigma A)\) at the identity basepoint for \(i\ge1\). The homology equality is \(H_i(S(\Sigma A)_0;\mathbb Z)\cong H_i(BGL(A);\mathbb Z)\). It is not generally the homology of all components together.''',r'''The scan's own identity-component annotation is retained and explains the restriction; it is not presented as wholly absent from the source. The direct-sum ellipsis is replaced by a homotopy-type statement, without asserting a canonical splitting of H-spaces. The right-hand group homology means homology of a classifying space.''','B',r'''The suspension and plus identifications are \citet[the second and third theorem blocks]{vaserstein1973foundations} and \citet[\S18 and Theorem 27.2]{vaserstein1976foundations}. The modern distinction between \(K_0(A)\times BGL(A)^+\) and its connected factor is explicit in \citet[IV, Definition 1.1.1]{weibel2013kbook}.''',r'''established with the component convention. Dropping it changes even \(H_0\), so the notebook's margin is mathematically significant.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations','weibel2013kbook'],match='partial',confidence='medium')
corr('P000422-P000423','speaker and date at the Quillen comparison block','Catalogue attributes a separate talk to Kazhdan on 20 March.','Preserve the catalogue metadata conditionally: this block has no separate dated Kazhdan heading; it may continue Vaserstein.','P422 and P423 scans inspected. Gelfand 20/III is explicit below the P423 divider; Kazhdan Proof begins only on P424.',typ='provenance')
corr('P000423','homology and K_0 components',r'\(H_iGL(\mu A)=H_iGL(MA)\).',r'On the left use the identity component of the simplicial realization; on the right use stable group homology.','The scan has an explicit identity-component annotation in the right margin. Vaserstein 1973 and Weibel IV Definition 1.1.1 separate K_0 components.',typ='ambiguity')
save()

talk(258,'1972-03-20','I. M. Gelfand','P000423-P000424','Quillen’s K-groups of finite fields and Frobenius',r'''Below an explicit divider and the heading ``20/III. Gelfand'', the notes ask for the K-groups of a finite field, state the low-degree groups and Quillen's general answer, and compare the algebraic closure with periodic K-theory. A brief continuation on P000424 mentions Frobenius and contains an unclear asymptotic remark. Kazhdan's named proof begins immediately afterward and belongs to record 259. The principal result is expository: it was already announced in Quillen's Nice ICM lecture in 1970 and printed in 1971, before the detailed 1972 Annals paper.''')
item(258,'P000423','theorem','The first three K-groups of a finite field',r'''For a finite field \(F_q\), the notes give \(K_0(F_q)=\mathbb Z\), \(K_1(F_q)=F_q^*\), and say that \(K_2(F_q)=0\) is easy to show.''',r'''Let \(q=p^a\) be a prime power. Dimension induces \(K_0(\mathbb F_q)\cong\mathbb Z\), determinant induces \(K_1(\mathbb F_q)\cong\mathbb F_q^\times\cong\mathbb Z/(q-1)\), and \(K_2(\mathbb F_q)=0\). The dimension and determinant identifications are canonical; an isomorphism of the multiplicative group with a chosen cyclic group requires choosing a generator.''',r'''The prime-power hypothesis and the distinction between canonical descriptions and generator choices are explicit. No unrecorded elementary proof of the K-two assertion is attributed to Gelfand.''','E',r'''The low-degree groups were already part of the theory of Bass, Steinberg and Milnor; Quillen's announcement explicitly says that his definition agrees with Bass and Milnor \citep[\S3, p.50]{quillen1971cohomology}. They also follow from its finite-field computation, stated at the 1970 ICM and printed in 1971.''',r'''classical and completely determined.''',refs=['quillen1971cohomology'],literal='correct')
item(258,'P000423','theorem','Quillen’s complete finite-field calculation',r'''The even groups vanish, and the odd groups are written as multiplicative groups of finite extension fields. The overwritten odd index admits both \(2i-1\) and \(2i+1\) readings, while the exponent placement on \(F_{q^i}\) is also uncertain.''',r'''For every prime power \(q\) and every integer \(i\ge1\), \[K_{2i}(\mathbb F_q)=0,\qquad K_{2i-1}(\mathbb F_q)\cong\mathbb Z/(q^i-1).\] In particular the odd group is abstractly isomorphic to \(\mathbb F_{q^i}^{\times}\). If indexed instead by \(2i+1\), its order is \(q^{i+1}-1\), not \(q^i-1\). These abstract cyclic-group descriptions by themselves do not specify the natural maps under field extensions.''',r'''The index is fixed by the verified theorem rather than by guessing the overwritten sign. The equation with a multiplicative field group is taken as an abstract group isomorphism, without a canonical or Frobenius-equivariant identification.''','E',r'''\citet[\S3, Theorem 1 and its consequences, p.51]{quillen1971cohomology} announces the Adams-fibre equivalence from which these groups follow. The first circulation was the Nice ICM in 1970; the proceedings were printed in 1971. \citet{quillen1972finitefields} is the later full Annals paper, whose publisher metadata was checked here. The proof mechanisms are covered in occurrence 1971-259.3, with Brauer lifting in 1971-259.5 and the Lang comparison in 1971-259.6.''',r'''completely resolved. The 1972 publication date must not be used to classify this March report as a later solution.''',refs=['quillen1971cohomology','quillen1972finitefields'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(258,'P000423','theorem','K-theory of the algebraic closure and its apparent periodicity',r'''The displayed formula over \(\overline F_q\) gives vanishing positive even groups and identifies odd groups with \(\overline F_q^*\), followed by ``like the periodicity theorem''. The first odd index is overwritten and provisionally \(2i+1\).''',r'''For \(k=\overline{\mathbb F}_p\), \(K_0(k)=\mathbb Z\), and for every \(i\ge1\), \[K_{2i}(k)=0,\qquad K_{2i-1}(k)\cong\bigoplus_{\ell\ne p}\mathbb Q_\ell/\mathbb Z_\ell.\] Each odd group is abstractly isomorphic to \(k^\times\). This similarity of abstract groups is not a canonical integral Bott-periodicity theorem, and does not identify the Frobenius actions in different odd degrees.''',r'''The explicit prime-to-p divisible group and the warning about Frobenius distinguish the actual calculation from an unrestricted periodicity assertion. Including \(i=1\) incorporates the separately displayed K-one group.''','E',r'''This is \citet[\S3, Theorem 2, p.51]{quillen1971cohomology}, already circulated in 1970 and published in 1971. The same theorem specifies the degree-dependent Frobenius action, which is lost if all odd groups are simply labeled \(k^\times\).''',r'''completely known. The notes' periodicity analogy is useful at the level of abstract abelian groups but insufficient for naturality.''',refs=['quillen1971cohomology'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(258,'P000424','method','Frobenius invariants recover the finite-field orders',r'''The continuation says only that the Frobenius automorphism is used.''',r'''For \(k=\overline{\mathbb F}_p\), arithmetic Frobenius \(x\mapsto x^p\) acts on \(K_{2i-1}(k)\) as multiplication by \(p^i\). Thus \(x\mapsto x^q\), with \(q=p^a\), acts as multiplication by \(q^i\). Extension of scalars identifies \(K_{2i-1}(\mathbb F_q)\) with the subgroup of Galois invariants in \(K_{2i-1}(k)\); this is the kernel of multiplication by \(q^i-1\), cyclic of that order. For finite intermediate extensions the restriction-of-scalars map corresponds to the norm on these invariant groups.''',r'''The action, invariant subgroup and norm statement are supplied from the matching theorem to explain the short reference to Frobenius. They are not asserted to have been written out in the seminar. Arithmetic Frobenius is fixed explicitly so that its inverse convention cannot reverse the action.''','E',r'''All the functorial assertions appear in \citet[\S3, Theorem 2, p.51]{quillen1971cohomology}. They sharpen the group-only formulas in the preceding two items and were already available before the lecture.''',r'''established. In degree \(2i-1\), identifying the group with \(k^\times\) while retaining the usual Frobenius power \(q\) would be wrong unless \(i=1\).''',refs=['quillen1971cohomology'],match='partial')
item(258,'P000424','remark','An unclear statement about increasing degree',r'''Before the Frobenius sentence, the notes say that as \(i\) increases the K-functor approaches an elementary K-functor, followed by an unreadable or abbreviated final word.''',r'''No limiting operation, comparison map, stable range, or precise elementary functor is specified. The assertion cannot be reconstructed uniquely as a theorem about increasing K-degree.''',r'''The nearby finite-field formulas and periodicity analogy do not supply the missing notion of approximation. No equivalence with Milnor K-theory or a stability theorem is substituted for the unclear wording.''','F',r'''The verified Quillen announcement explains the surrounding calculation but does not identify this sentence. Searches of Quillen's finite-field and Segal-related accounts did not recover a matching degree-asymptotic assertion with the omitted parameters.''',r'''unidentified from this brief continuation. It is not evidence for a presently open stabilization problem.''',refs=['quillen1971cohomology'],literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low')
corr('P000423','finite-field odd degree and field exponent',r'Overwritten \(K_{2i\pm1}(F_q)\), with right side provisionally \(F_{q^i}^*\).',r'The verified theorem is \(K_{2i-1}(F_q)\cong\mathbb Z/(q^i-1)\); equivalently \(K_{2i+1}(F_q)\cong\mathbb Z/(q^{i+1}-1)\).','Scan inspected; the overwritten sign remains ambiguous. Quillen ICM §3 Theorems 1–2 determine the mathematical indexing.',typ='ambiguity')
corr('P000423-P000424','chronology of the finite-field result','The prominent full publication is Quillen 1972.','The theorem was announced at the 1970 ICM and printed in its 1971 proceedings, before Gelfand’s March 1972 report.','Quillen Cohomology of groups, §3 Theorems 1–2, pp.50–51, primary text opened; Annals metadata independently checked.',typ='provenance')
save()

# Final distinct-item split: preserve every earlier identifier, especially 246.9.
_central=next(x for x in items if x['id']=='1971-246.12')
_central.update(kind='theorem',title='Perfect groups, universal central extensions, and second homology',notes=r'''The middle of P000399 records \(H_2(G,\mathbb Z)\to\widetilde G\to G\) with the condition \([G,G]=G\), following a Steinberg-type extension for the elementary classical group.''',statement=r'''A group \(G\) admits a universal central extension if and only if it is perfect. Its kernel is naturally \(H_2(G;\mathbb Z)\). For the stable elementary linear group of an associative unital ring \(A\), the Steinberg group \(\operatorname{St}(A)\to E(A)\) is its universal central extension, with central kernel \(K_2(A)\cong H_2(E(A);\mathbb Z)\).''',reconstruction=r'''The general perfect-group theorem is read separately from the proposed hermitian model. The verified stable linear case is a comparator; it does not by itself prove the same presentation theorem for every form parameter or every finite-rank classical group.''',**{'class':'E'},fate=r'''The relation with perfect groups and the classical Steinberg--Milnor construction predates the lecture. \citet[historical introduction, p.95; \S6, Theorem 6.1]{vaserstein1976foundations} gives the central-extension theorem and dates the field case to Steinberg's 1962 work; the modern general formulation is also treated in \citet[III, \S5]{weibel2013kbook}.''',present_status='As of November 2026: established for perfect groups and for the stated stable linear group. The additional hermitian and simplicial claims are separated in the next item.',literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',publication_match='partial',verification='read the result',refs=['vaserstein1976foundations','weibel2013kbook'],confidence='high')
item(246,'P000399','construction','The incompletely specified higher hermitian simplicial model',r'''The page writes \(0\to K_2O(A)\to\operatorname{St}O(A)\to EO(A)\to0\), calls it a universal central extension, and proposes \(K_nO(A)=\pi_{n-1}O(A)\). A question asks which finite subsets should be simplices; triangular unipotent matrices and their left and right translates are sketched. The ordinary linear analogue appears in the margin.''',r'''A precise higher hermitian construction must specify \((A,*,\varepsilon,\Lambda)\), the stable elementary unitary subgroup, its generators and root relations, the admissible triangular subgroups, and the simplicial realization whose homotopy groups are intended. The surviving sketches do not determine all these data or establish comparison with a particular modern Grothendieck--Witt spectrum. The notation \(\pi_{n-1}O(A)\) cannot mean homotopy of the discrete group alone.''',r'''The general perfect-group theorem is retained separately in 1971-246.12. The ordinary Volodin model explains the method, but importing its linear comparison theorem does not identify the complete hermitian theory suggested here.''','F',r'''The prior stable form-group framework is \citet{vaserstein1970stabilization}; the linear simplicial method was published in \citet{volodin1971extraordinary}. Searches of Vaserstein's subsequent foundations papers found the ordinary associative-ring comparison \citep{vaserstein1973foundations,vaserstein1976foundations}, not a uniquely matching all-parameter hermitian statement for this sketch.''',r'''the proposed model remains underdetermined by the notes. This classification concerns identification, not a claim that modern hermitian K-theory remains undeveloped.''',refs=['vaserstein1970stabilization','volodin1971extraordinary','vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',area='Hermitian K-theory')
item(253,'P000414','conjecture','Vaserstein’s explicit expectation of agreement with Quillen',r'''After the pullback construction, the notebook says \ru{По мнению Л. В. теория Квиллена совпадает с этими}: in Vaserstein's opinion Quillen's theory agrees with these. The next line explicitly excludes Karoubi's polynomial theory.''',r'''For an associative ring \(A\) with identity, the stable Volodin--Vaserstein simplicial groups and the free-resolution groups of Swan and Gersten should be naturally isomorphic to \(K_n^Q(A)=\pi_nBGL(A)^+\) for every \(n\ge1\). The comparison does not assert agreement with Karoubi--Villamayor groups for arbitrary singular rings.''',r'''The positive index range and unital hypothesis identify the exact theorem later published. The notation does not make an unrestricted assertion about all nonunital or polynomial-homotopy constructions.''','A',r'''The expectation is stated as a theorem in the comparison block assigned conditionally to record 257. \citet[first theorem, p.231]{vaserstein1973foundations} announces the agreement, and \citet[\S27, Theorem 27.2; \S29, Theorem 29.1]{vaserstein1976foundations} gives the detailed comparisons. The latter explicitly dates first announcement to Gelfand's seminar in spring 1972; the short article was received on 14 November 1972.''',r'''resolved in the stated scope. GP-1971-21 links this conjectural wording, the suspension question in 1971-256.3, and the comparison theorem in 1971-257.2.''',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'],problem='GP-1971-21',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')

problems.extend([
 dict(id='GP-1971-20',title='Normalized universal relations among elementary matrices',statement=r'''Let \(R=\mathbb Z\langle X\rangle\) be the free associative unital ring, augmented to \(\mathbb Z\) by \(X\mapsto0\). Does \(\ker(K_2(R)\to K_2(\mathbb Z))\) vanish? Equivalently, is every universally valid stable elementary matrix relation whose Steinberg lift specializes to the identity under augmentation a consequence of the Steinberg relations? The normalization is necessary; arbitrary constant relations include the nonzero \(K_2(\mathbb Z)\).''',occurrences=['1971-247.5','1971-253.4','1971-256.5'],status=r'''As of November 2026: resolved in the stated augmented formulation. Vaserstein announces the answer in the March 1972 notebook and the free-ring theorem in \citet[second theorem]{vaserstein1973foundations}; \citet[Theorem 28.1 and Corollary 28.1]{vaserstein1976foundations} gives its precise reduced formulation. The February notebook omits the augmentation condition, so the match is qualified rather than an assertion that every unnormalized relation is trivial. No exact earlier recurrence was found in the supplied prior index.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations']),
 dict(id='GP-1971-21',title='Agreement of the early higher algebraic K-theory constructions',statement=r'''For every associative unital ring \(A\) and every \(n\ge1\), do the stable simplicial constructions of Volodin and Vaserstein and the free-resolution constructions of Swan and Gersten agree naturally with \(\pi_nBGL(A)^+\)? One concrete consequence asked in the notes is the Quillen-theory suspension isomorphism \(K_{n+1}(\Sigma A)\cong K_n(A)\). Polynomial-homotopy Karoubi--Villamayor groups are excluded without additional regularity.''',occurrences=['1971-253.14','1971-256.3','1971-257.2'],status=r'''As of November 2026: resolved for the specified stable constructions and unital rings. The spring 1972 seminar announcement is documented by \citet[introduction, p.97]{vaserstein1976foundations}; \citet[first theorem]{vaserstein1973foundations} publishes the comparison, with the detailed Quillen and free-resolution arguments in the 1976 paper, \S\S27--29. Record 257's catalogue attribution is conditional and does not create an additional independent priority claim. No exact earlier problem identifier was located in the supplied index.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['vaserstein1973foundations','vaserstein1976foundations'])
])

